00:08:53.129 --> 00:08:56.871
So the historical backdrop
of our inquiry are

00:08:56.871 --> 00:09:00.600
two East Asian countries
vying for regional dominance.

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:03.780
Japan's preeminence as the
regions exporter has waned

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:06.290
a little since its
post-World War II heyday,

00:09:06.290 --> 00:09:08.310
but observers note that Japan is building

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:10.550
its coffers of another product:

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:13.570
it's "gross national
coolness," we'll call it.

00:09:13.570 --> 00:09:15.377
In his long reign in office,

00:09:15.377 --> 00:09:19.410
former Prime Minister Abe
made a "Cool Japan" program

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:21.640
a multi-ministry effort coordinated

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:22.942
by the cabinet office

00:09:22.942 --> 00:09:26.500
with specific goals like
increasing trade and tourism,

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:27.600
product purchases,

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:29.210
attracting foreign public opinion

00:09:29.210 --> 00:09:31.720
for foreign policy and so on.

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:33.340
And we picked this picture

00:09:33.340 --> 00:09:35.120
as perhaps an emblematic moment

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:38.450
at the 2016 Rio Summer
Olympics closing ceremony,

00:09:38.450 --> 00:09:39.940
the handover ceremony,

00:09:39.940 --> 00:09:42.090
where there was a video montage

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:43.290
with Japanese athletes,

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:44.400
Hello Kitty,

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:45.780
classic manga characters

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:48.290
and Prime Minister Abe
stuck in Tokyo traffic

00:09:48.290 --> 00:09:49.320
in the beginning saying

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:51.030
he won't make it to Rio on time,

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:53.140
but with the help of Super Mario,

00:09:53.140 --> 00:09:55.000
the video game, and his friends,

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:56.990
he teleports through a pipe and pops up

00:09:56.990 --> 00:10:00.420
in the middle of the
stadium at Rio as Mario.

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:02.150
A viral moment, but for us,

00:10:02.150 --> 00:10:07.079
the point being that, even
the softer side of soft power,

00:10:07.079 --> 00:10:09.340
animation, cuisine,

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:10.610
it's not just bottom up

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:13.140
and political leaders
are actively harnessing

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:15.890
these resources for promotion and policy

00:10:15.890 --> 00:10:17.153
for national gains.

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:22.010
And Chinese leaders have thrown their hats

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:23.290
in the ring as well.

00:10:23.290 --> 00:10:25.887
Here are some quotes
from former President Hu;

00:10:25.887 --> 00:10:29.830
"We must enhance culture
as part of soft power."

00:10:29.830 --> 00:10:31.117
And President Xi;

00:10:31.117 --> 00:10:33.967
"The vision is that Chinese
cultural soft power has grown

00:10:33.967 --> 00:10:34.800
"much stronger."

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:37.513
So this is in the CCP Congress sessions.

00:10:38.388 --> 00:10:42.010
So with the aim of winning
this public opinion war,

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:44.441
the Chinese Communist
Party sees increasing

00:10:44.441 --> 00:10:48.030
the country's soft power
as a core national goal,

00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:50.320
and has spent lavishly on efforts

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:52.770
like the Belt and Road Initiative as well.

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:56.183
So in this new era of
soft power competition,

00:10:57.080 --> 00:10:58.440
our question is,

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:02.500
does soft power influence
global attitudes?

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:04.880
What soft power resources work

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:06.620
and under what conditions?

00:11:06.620 --> 00:11:08.930
So right now, I'm spending
a quick three slides

00:11:08.930 --> 00:11:09.790
on an overview,

00:11:09.790 --> 00:11:12.690
and then I'll go a little
deeper into the theory ,

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:15.210
and then Colin and Chang will takeover

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:16.900
for case selection and design,

00:11:16.900 --> 00:11:19.523
and I'll do some closing remarks.

00:11:20.900 --> 00:11:24.630
So there's robust literature

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:29.070
on soft power in political science.

00:11:29.070 --> 00:11:30.360
And building on Nye,

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:33.100
we structure our theoretical framework

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:36.013
with three key soft power resources.

00:11:36.013 --> 00:11:37.670
So the first is culture;

00:11:37.670 --> 00:11:39.330
things like art and drama.

00:11:39.330 --> 00:11:41.110
The second one is political values,

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:43.130
such as freedom of speech

00:11:43.130 --> 00:11:44.550
and a responsible government,

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:46.480
bill of rights and so forth.

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:48.650
And finally, foreign policy.

00:11:48.650 --> 00:11:51.330
Another country's official
international relations policy

00:11:51.330 --> 00:11:52.823
with one's home country.

00:11:57.850 --> 00:12:00.530
So with this framework,

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:03.530
our main theory is as old...

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:05.480
The insight is practically as old

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:07.890
as the concept of soft power itself

00:12:07.890 --> 00:12:11.750
but it's that domestic
political context matters.

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:13.770
So more specifically,

00:12:13.770 --> 00:12:17.127
when the bilateral
relations are depoliticized

00:12:17.127 --> 00:12:19.550
in the domestic political arena,

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:21.675
soft power efforts can work.

00:12:21.675 --> 00:12:26.050
But when bilateral
relations are politicized

00:12:26.050 --> 00:12:27.580
in domestic politics,

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:29.063
they can backfire.

00:12:31.481 --> 00:12:32.314
Excuse me.

00:12:35.222 --> 00:12:36.222
There we go.

00:12:39.310 --> 00:12:40.930
Our main result...

00:12:40.930 --> 00:12:41.763
Rather, sorry.

00:12:41.763 --> 00:12:42.910
Our research design.

00:12:42.910 --> 00:12:45.270
So we use a survey experiment setup

00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:49.200
and we expose the audience to
soft power promotion efforts

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:52.440
by China and Japan in two key cases.

00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:54.057
So South Korea first,

00:12:54.057 --> 00:12:56.580
where the South Korea relationship...

00:12:56.580 --> 00:12:58.880
Oh sorry, South Korea-Japan relationship

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:02.610
and the South Korea-China
relationship are politicized

00:13:02.610 --> 00:13:04.053
in domestic politics.

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:11.270
And Malaysia, where the
Malaysia-Japan relationship

00:13:11.270 --> 00:13:13.770
and the Malaysia-China relationship are

00:13:13.770 --> 00:13:15.900
relatively depoliticized.

00:13:17.810 --> 00:13:21.410
Our key results are that
soft power can be effective

00:13:21.410 --> 00:13:23.680
in non-politicized environments

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:27.800
but backfires in politicized environments.

00:13:27.800 --> 00:13:29.840
So we find that in Malaysia,

00:13:29.840 --> 00:13:31.840
soft power efforts succeeded

00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:34.040
in changing cooperative attitudes towards

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:36.530
each of the countries
promoting soft power,

00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:39.193
but in South Korea, they backfired.

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:42.620
Our second point is that content matters.

00:13:42.620 --> 00:13:44.090
Soft power research has showed

00:13:44.090 --> 00:13:47.810
similar patterns of
effectiveness across contexts.

00:13:47.810 --> 00:13:51.150
So for instance, foreign aid
and culture were more effective

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:56.077
in moving respondents willingness
to cooperate favorably,

00:13:56.077 --> 00:14:00.620
whereas treatments showing
political values were

00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:02.130
less effective.

00:14:02.130 --> 00:14:03.420
And we'll discuss this later,

00:14:03.420 --> 00:14:05.840
whether it was because of our setup

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:08.950
or because of various factors,

00:14:08.950 --> 00:14:10.340
we'll talk about later.

00:14:10.340 --> 00:14:12.800
The theoretical payoffs of our paper.

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:14.800
We theorize and test soft powers effects

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:18.030
across these three core
soft power resources.

00:14:18.030 --> 00:14:19.920
We show how aid influence attitudes

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:21.830
in non-recipient countries

00:14:21.830 --> 00:14:25.660
and we contribute to the
literature on public diplomacy.

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:28.708
So diving in further into our theory.

00:14:28.708 --> 00:14:32.823
So there are three types
of soft power resources.

00:14:33.750 --> 00:14:36.410
The first one is culture,

00:14:36.410 --> 00:14:41.410
so these are things such as art,

00:14:41.410 --> 00:14:45.190
literature, various cuisine and so forth,

00:14:45.190 --> 00:14:49.850
that increases the attractiveness
of a country's profile

00:14:49.850 --> 00:14:52.530
to the recipient individual.

00:14:52.530 --> 00:14:55.300
This is an image of Pokemon characters

00:14:55.300 --> 00:15:00.300
and Son-Goku animation
character from Japan

00:15:01.712 --> 00:15:03.803
at the Macy's Thanksgiving day parade.

00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:07.160
Foreign policy, theoretically,

00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:10.310
is a host of different policy initiatives.

00:15:10.310 --> 00:15:15.140
In our study today, we
focus on international aid.

00:15:15.140 --> 00:15:18.053
This is a picture of ODA
being delivered from Japan.

00:15:19.060 --> 00:15:20.700
And political values,

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:25.700
so this has been long
established in the literature,

00:15:26.620 --> 00:15:29.130
originally by Nye and others,

00:15:29.130 --> 00:15:34.130
as emblematic from the US'
robust democratic liberalism.

00:15:36.430 --> 00:15:41.430
This picture is from the 1960s
US-Japan security protests

00:15:41.710 --> 00:15:42.543
in Japan.

00:15:42.543 --> 00:15:44.050
So that's the diet building

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:46.000
and that's all those people protesting.

00:15:48.190 --> 00:15:51.450
So our theory from the individual level.

00:15:51.450 --> 00:15:55.750
So on the right, here is kind
of the original environment

00:15:58.660 --> 00:16:00.610
in which an individual is operating.

00:16:00.610 --> 00:16:03.180
So this is a low politicization country,

00:16:03.180 --> 00:16:04.930
say it's country X.

00:16:04.930 --> 00:16:07.200
And this is a treatment,

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:09.930
a dosage of any soft power resource.

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:12.338
So let's say this is Japan...

00:16:12.338 --> 00:16:14.980
Let's say this is a documentary

00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:17.640
on Japanese beverage making

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:21.330
and its origins of whiskey, for instance.

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:25.230
And when the environment
is low politicized...

00:16:25.230 --> 00:16:28.550
Not as politicized to begin with,

00:16:28.550 --> 00:16:30.610
it's more or less a blank slate

00:16:30.610 --> 00:16:32.300
or less politicized slate.

00:16:32.300 --> 00:16:35.260
So the individual sees this, is exposed,

00:16:35.260 --> 00:16:37.610
they have updates about their beliefs

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:38.880
about the foreign nation

00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:41.563
and its trustworthiness and coolness,

00:16:41.563 --> 00:16:45.440
and we hypothesized that
it increases support

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:48.230
for security and/or
economic relationships.

00:16:48.230 --> 00:16:52.110
So next time they're out
for a gift for a friend,

00:16:52.110 --> 00:16:53.940
they might look for a Japanese whiskey

00:16:53.940 --> 00:16:55.373
rather than something else.

00:16:56.470 --> 00:16:57.730
On the other hand,

00:16:57.730 --> 00:16:59.300
this is another country,

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:03.140
Country Y in a highly
politicized environment.

00:17:03.140 --> 00:17:05.240
And again, they might get exposure

00:17:05.240 --> 00:17:07.060
to a soft power resource.

00:17:07.060 --> 00:17:08.470
But in this case,

00:17:08.470 --> 00:17:10.240
the mechanism is a little bit different

00:17:10.240 --> 00:17:13.048
because they already have
priors about the country.

00:17:13.048 --> 00:17:17.120
And what happens is rather
than updating information,

00:17:17.120 --> 00:17:18.486
it's more of a prime

00:17:18.486 --> 00:17:22.010
that associates back to their already held

00:17:22.010 --> 00:17:24.230
negative emotional reactions,

00:17:24.230 --> 00:17:26.440
which lead to less support

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:29.330
for security and economic relationships.

00:17:29.330 --> 00:17:34.250
So now, a note for this slide is that

00:17:34.250 --> 00:17:37.900
it's not necessarily that
a priming is going to lead

00:17:37.900 --> 00:17:40.000
to less cooperative behavior

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:41.790
in terms of the bilateral relationship,

00:17:41.790 --> 00:17:44.550
it's that empirically, we find that...

00:17:44.550 --> 00:17:48.590
The literature finds and
we work within a framework

00:17:48.590 --> 00:17:51.040
where more countries, when they politicize

00:17:51.040 --> 00:17:52.641
a bilateral relationship,

00:17:52.641 --> 00:17:54.560
it's more antagonistic

00:17:54.560 --> 00:17:58.610
and in a negative sense than
a positive one, unfortunately.

00:17:58.610 --> 00:18:01.300
And so, that's why when they get primed

00:18:01.300 --> 00:18:03.180
about a politicized relationship,

00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:04.290
the result is negative.

00:18:04.290 --> 00:18:07.843
It's not necessarily about
having the prime or not itself.

00:18:09.028 --> 00:18:12.103
All right, so moving on
to case selection, Colin.

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:16.370
- Okay, so with full understanding

00:18:16.370 --> 00:18:20.655
that this panel is awash
with experts on the region,

00:18:20.655 --> 00:18:22.850
I'm going to hazard an introduction

00:18:22.850 --> 00:18:24.650
of our soft power senders.

00:18:24.650 --> 00:18:26.380
These are the two countries really vying

00:18:26.380 --> 00:18:28.730
for influence through soft power

00:18:28.730 --> 00:18:30.890
in East Asia and abroad.

00:18:30.890 --> 00:18:32.530
So they have a fairly
distinct characteristics.

00:18:32.530 --> 00:18:34.750
I think everybody here
is probably familiar

00:18:34.750 --> 00:18:37.250
with Japan has relatively successful,

00:18:37.250 --> 00:18:38.940
soft cultural power,

00:18:38.940 --> 00:18:41.417
and its economic success
is very well known.

00:18:41.417 --> 00:18:44.210
Japanese government has spent quite a lot

00:18:44.210 --> 00:18:45.630
on developmental assistance

00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:46.660
and foreign aid programs

00:18:46.660 --> 00:18:48.680
throughout the post-war era.

00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:50.700
Chinese cultural power
has considerable reach,

00:18:50.700 --> 00:18:52.470
depending on the content type and market,

00:18:52.470 --> 00:18:55.930
and the government emphasis
on this is increasing.

00:18:55.930 --> 00:18:57.220
Additionally China's growth rate

00:18:57.220 --> 00:18:59.330
over the past few decades has gotten it

00:18:59.330 --> 00:19:00.790
the sort of attention, I think,

00:19:00.790 --> 00:19:04.150
that Japan received for
the economic miracle.

00:19:04.150 --> 00:19:06.640
And recently, we've seen huge,

00:19:06.640 --> 00:19:08.550
highly publicized developments,

00:19:08.550 --> 00:19:10.460
like the One Belt One Road Initiative,

00:19:10.460 --> 00:19:14.240
which are sort of meant to
showcase Chinese assistance

00:19:14.240 --> 00:19:17.040
within a broader foreign policy framework.

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:20.073
So now we'll quickly run
through the dyads of our cases.

00:19:21.330 --> 00:19:23.430
So up top, I should note
that there's not really

00:19:23.430 --> 00:19:25.970
a perfectly politicized
or de-politicized case

00:19:25.970 --> 00:19:27.590
to do this experiment.

00:19:27.590 --> 00:19:31.080
We made our assessments on
which countries might work best

00:19:31.080 --> 00:19:32.450
as cases from the background

00:19:32.450 --> 00:19:33.750
on the theoretical dimensions

00:19:33.750 --> 00:19:35.610
and survey data from over the years

00:19:35.610 --> 00:19:37.730
about favoribility toward Japan and China,

00:19:37.730 --> 00:19:40.310
and we'll see more on that data shortly.

00:19:40.310 --> 00:19:43.330
But first up, the Japan-South
Korea relationship has been

00:19:43.330 --> 00:19:45.556
pretty fraught for a very long time.

00:19:45.556 --> 00:19:49.040
So despite a shared status
as developed democracies,

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:51.627
we see highly politicized
bilateral relations

00:19:51.627 --> 00:19:53.280
because of historical factors

00:19:53.280 --> 00:19:55.762
like wars, colonial legacy,

00:19:55.762 --> 00:19:58.350
and more current pressing issues,

00:19:58.350 --> 00:20:01.880
like security cooperation
and territorial disputes.

00:20:01.880 --> 00:20:04.530
South Korea is not really a recipient

00:20:04.530 --> 00:20:05.383
of Japanese aid

00:20:05.383 --> 00:20:07.410
and Japanese cultural goods are

00:20:07.410 --> 00:20:10.194
often targeted in South Korean boycotts.

00:20:10.194 --> 00:20:13.984
Malaysia, in this case, is non-politicized

00:20:13.984 --> 00:20:18.690
with Japan since bilateral
relations don't really feature

00:20:18.690 --> 00:20:21.340
in domestic politics of Malaysia.

00:20:21.340 --> 00:20:24.410
So the Malaysians are
consumers of Japanese products.

00:20:24.410 --> 00:20:26.120
They've also received considerable funding

00:20:26.120 --> 00:20:28.330
and technical assistance from Japan,

00:20:28.330 --> 00:20:30.013
since the 1960s.

00:20:31.900 --> 00:20:34.360
Moving onto the China side,

00:20:34.360 --> 00:20:38.730
we see, I think, a different sort of case

00:20:38.730 --> 00:20:40.790
with the China-South Korea relationship.

00:20:40.790 --> 00:20:43.280
It's politicized for
very different reasons.

00:20:43.280 --> 00:20:45.850
The primary clash here
between these countries is

00:20:45.850 --> 00:20:48.417
over political issues and security issues.

00:20:48.417 --> 00:20:52.170
And so, we've seen disputes
over human rights issues,

00:20:52.170 --> 00:20:53.400
mischaracterizations of

00:20:53.400 --> 00:20:55.301
the South Korean government's positions,

00:20:55.301 --> 00:20:57.210
and the Chinese relationship

00:20:57.210 --> 00:21:00.502
with North Korea can also cause issues.

00:21:00.502 --> 00:21:03.140
So again, South Korea is
not really a recipient

00:21:03.140 --> 00:21:04.880
of Chinese aid, of course.

00:21:04.880 --> 00:21:08.370
And although, South Koreans
do consume Chinese goods

00:21:08.370 --> 00:21:09.510
and cultural products,

00:21:09.510 --> 00:21:12.343
I think that's the extent
of the relationship there.

00:21:13.203 --> 00:21:15.560
Malaysia, once again, has little

00:21:15.560 --> 00:21:17.960
or no politicization on
relations with China.

00:21:17.960 --> 00:21:19.380
So it's a recipient of loans,

00:21:19.380 --> 00:21:21.183
as part of BRI.

00:21:22.395 --> 00:21:24.580
We have seen a willingness to be selective

00:21:24.580 --> 00:21:28.010
about which projects the
Malaysian government wants

00:21:28.010 --> 00:21:29.180
to accept as part of

00:21:29.180 --> 00:21:31.520
the One Belt One Road Initiative.

00:21:31.520 --> 00:21:34.150
There's also a large ethnic
Chinese minority in Malaysia

00:21:34.150 --> 00:21:36.500
but the domestic racial cleavages haven't

00:21:36.500 --> 00:21:37.660
really spilled over into

00:21:37.660 --> 00:21:39.513
the bilateral relations with China.

00:21:40.630 --> 00:21:43.820
So moving on, I think we
have a two-by-two here

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:46.300
that lays out, in the
simplest terms I think,

00:21:46.300 --> 00:21:48.310
what our expectations should be based

00:21:48.310 --> 00:21:52.203
on the assessments of these
dyads politicization levels.

00:21:53.160 --> 00:21:56.772
So the Japan-South Korea
relationship is highly politicized,

00:21:56.772 --> 00:22:00.560
which suggests that we would
have a backlash effect.

00:22:00.560 --> 00:22:02.530
In the China-South Korea dyad,

00:22:02.530 --> 00:22:04.500
we see relatively less politicization

00:22:04.500 --> 00:22:06.220
but it still exists,

00:22:06.220 --> 00:22:07.810
and so there's perhaps an opportunity

00:22:07.810 --> 00:22:10.250
for within case variation among

00:22:10.250 --> 00:22:11.839
the South Korea respondents

00:22:11.839 --> 00:22:14.373
versus the Japan case.

00:22:15.540 --> 00:22:17.440
Malaysia-Japan isn't politicized,

00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:18.470
so we should hopefully see

00:22:18.470 --> 00:22:20.170
some positive effects from treatments.

00:22:20.170 --> 00:22:22.914
And the same goes for Malaysia-China.

00:22:22.914 --> 00:22:25.250
So all in all, I think you can see,

00:22:25.250 --> 00:22:27.740
we expect to see soft power efforts

00:22:27.740 --> 00:22:29.640
by both countries doing better in Malaysia

00:22:29.640 --> 00:22:31.050
than in South Korea.

00:22:31.050 --> 00:22:33.190
And then, we might expect
to see some variation

00:22:33.190 --> 00:22:35.863
among the South Korea respondent groups.

00:22:37.510 --> 00:22:40.150
Okay, so this is a feeling thermometer

00:22:40.150 --> 00:22:41.390
that we took from our respondents

00:22:41.390 --> 00:22:45.600
to visualize and justify
that set of expectations.

00:22:45.600 --> 00:22:46.470
So what you're seeing here

00:22:46.470 --> 00:22:48.843
on the Y-axis is number of respondents,

00:22:48.843 --> 00:22:51.250
and the X axis is feelings

00:22:51.250 --> 00:22:52.510
with 10 being most positive.

00:22:52.510 --> 00:22:55.320
So what we have here
is like a density plot

00:22:55.320 --> 00:22:57.560
of feelings towards the countries.

00:22:57.560 --> 00:22:59.400
And as you can see, the
South Korean respondents are

00:22:59.400 --> 00:23:00.420
really quite negative

00:23:00.420 --> 00:23:02.710
about both Japan and China,

00:23:02.710 --> 00:23:04.770
whereas the Malaysian respondents are

00:23:04.770 --> 00:23:08.615
pretty much more positive
about both countries.

00:23:08.615 --> 00:23:12.313
And now, we'll take a look at the surveys.

00:23:13.750 --> 00:23:15.630
So we fielded two surveys,

00:23:15.630 --> 00:23:18.110
one in South Korea and one in Malaysia.

00:23:18.110 --> 00:23:21.910
Each survey was completed in
the primary local language,

00:23:21.910 --> 00:23:24.380
so Korean and Malay respectively.

00:23:24.380 --> 00:23:26.760
Our first wave went out early this month.

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:28.580
We just finished our
second wave this week,

00:23:28.580 --> 00:23:29.950
which followed up with respondents

00:23:29.950 --> 00:23:31.830
between five and seven
days after completion

00:23:31.830 --> 00:23:33.490
of the first wave.

00:23:33.490 --> 00:23:35.070
And our intent for that second wave is

00:23:35.070 --> 00:23:36.150
really to interrogate whether

00:23:36.150 --> 00:23:37.850
these treatment effects are sticky.

00:23:37.850 --> 00:23:40.430
So the results didn't make it

00:23:40.430 --> 00:23:42.110
into the paper because
of time considerations

00:23:42.110 --> 00:23:45.140
but they will be going
into future iterations.

00:23:45.140 --> 00:23:47.102
We hope you stay tuned.

00:23:47.102 --> 00:23:49.670
So the surveys were
administered by Dynata,

00:23:49.670 --> 00:23:51.950
which is an online market
research company operating

00:23:51.950 --> 00:23:54.720
in both South Korea and Malaysia.

00:23:54.720 --> 00:23:56.960
For sampling, we stratified age and gender

00:23:56.960 --> 00:23:58.390
to best approximate the sample

00:23:58.390 --> 00:23:59.650
to the broader populations

00:23:59.650 --> 00:24:02.876
and as a result, the Malaysian
sample we got matches,

00:24:02.876 --> 00:24:04.968
more or less, on both age and gender.

00:24:04.968 --> 00:24:07.770
The South Korean sample matches on gender

00:24:07.770 --> 00:24:11.140
and skews just ever so
slightly young compared

00:24:11.140 --> 00:24:13.133
with the broader population.

00:24:14.010 --> 00:24:16.490
And so, for all these
screenings gave us samples

00:24:16.490 --> 00:24:18.960
of 2,740 in South Korea

00:24:18.960 --> 00:24:22.433
and 2,716 in Malaysia.

00:24:23.798 --> 00:24:27.337
So we have three soft
power treatment groups

00:24:27.337 --> 00:24:29.320
and a control group in both countries.

00:24:29.320 --> 00:24:31.500
So the three soft power
treatment groups are meant

00:24:31.500 --> 00:24:33.490
to track the theoretical categories

00:24:33.490 --> 00:24:34.323
of soft power,

00:24:34.323 --> 00:24:36.270
so cultural products on

00:24:36.270 --> 00:24:37.660
the culture dimension,

00:24:37.660 --> 00:24:40.767
politics as operationalized by protest,

00:24:40.767 --> 00:24:43.120
and foreign policy here is in

00:24:43.120 --> 00:24:45.220
the form of foreign aid.

00:24:45.220 --> 00:24:47.640
So our two soft power senders or diffusers

00:24:47.640 --> 00:24:50.060
in East Asia being Japan and China,

00:24:50.060 --> 00:24:51.440
we used video treatments

00:24:51.440 --> 00:24:53.853
rather than a conventional vignettes.

00:24:53.853 --> 00:24:57.610
And this was difficult,

00:24:57.610 --> 00:24:59.140
finding comparable video treatments

00:24:59.140 --> 00:25:02.524
between Japan and China
to comparable messaging,

00:25:02.524 --> 00:25:04.100
was a challenge.

00:25:04.100 --> 00:25:06.510
We think that using the
real video treatments

00:25:06.510 --> 00:25:08.040
better approximates how people

00:25:08.040 --> 00:25:09.960
in South Korea and Malaysia are going

00:25:09.960 --> 00:25:11.966
to encounter soft power.

00:25:11.966 --> 00:25:14.270
So these videos are drawn
from fictional media

00:25:14.270 --> 00:25:16.093
and news clips from each country.

00:25:17.070 --> 00:25:19.537
And we would love to have
our respondents watch

00:25:19.537 --> 00:25:21.754
"My Neighbor Totoro" in its entirety

00:25:21.754 --> 00:25:25.560
but unfortunately, time
and money are both limited,

00:25:25.560 --> 00:25:28.650
so treatments are limited
to only a few minutes.

00:25:28.650 --> 00:25:30.850
And we have, in total,

00:25:30.850 --> 00:25:33.180
a three by two factorial
design with the control.

00:25:33.180 --> 00:25:36.098
So ending up with seven groups in total.

00:25:36.098 --> 00:25:38.270
Respondents were randomly assigned

00:25:38.270 --> 00:25:41.040
to the groups and watched
at least 100 seconds

00:25:41.040 --> 00:25:42.870
of the corresponding video treatment.

00:25:42.870 --> 00:25:45.450
So to go into a little
more detail on that,

00:25:45.450 --> 00:25:47.497
I will hand it over to Changwook.

00:25:49.220 --> 00:25:50.190
- Oh, thanks Hikaru,

00:25:50.190 --> 00:25:51.590
and thanks Colin.

00:25:51.590 --> 00:25:53.360
Since our seminar participants didn't have

00:25:53.360 --> 00:25:56.090
a chance to see the
actual treatment videos,

00:25:56.090 --> 00:25:57.543
let me briefly describe them.

00:25:58.640 --> 00:25:59.910
For the control video,

00:25:59.910 --> 00:26:02.720
we featured nature and flowers,

00:26:02.720 --> 00:26:05.000
which we think are unrelated to soft power

00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:06.790
in Japan and China.

00:26:06.790 --> 00:26:09.179
For Japanese cultural soft power,

00:26:09.179 --> 00:26:11.610
we chose a popular Japanese anime,

00:26:11.610 --> 00:26:13.457
entitled "My Neighbor Totoro."

00:26:14.350 --> 00:26:16.170
For Japanese cultural soft power,

00:26:16.170 --> 00:26:17.530
we chose a video showing

00:26:17.530 --> 00:26:20.570
how Japanese citizens
demonstrate their grievances

00:26:20.570 --> 00:26:22.400
toward their government,

00:26:22.400 --> 00:26:25.753
especially towards Former
Prime Minister Abe.

00:26:25.753 --> 00:26:28.630
For the Japanese foreign aid treatment,

00:26:28.630 --> 00:26:32.590
we chose a video of MOFA
promoting Japanese contribution

00:26:32.590 --> 00:26:34.000
to nation-building projects

00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:35.952
in developing countries.

00:26:35.952 --> 00:26:37.970
The video focuses on foreign aid

00:26:37.970 --> 00:26:41.000
for countries other than
South Korea and Malaysia.

00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:43.070
So it suggests the general foreign policy,

00:26:43.070 --> 00:26:47.054
not the direct payment or
benefit to the target countries.

00:26:47.054 --> 00:26:50.321
Moving on to Chinese soft power treatment,

00:26:50.321 --> 00:26:51.810
for comparability issue,

00:26:51.810 --> 00:26:54.900
the premier selections
for Chinese soft power

00:26:54.900 --> 00:26:56.430
largely echoed the selections for

00:26:56.430 --> 00:26:57.653
the Japanese soft power.

00:26:58.490 --> 00:27:00.130
With same control video,

00:27:00.130 --> 00:27:01.610
we use another media clip from

00:27:01.610 --> 00:27:03.670
a popular Chinese historical drama,

00:27:03.670 --> 00:27:06.057
entitled "The Longest Day in China."

00:27:08.127 --> 00:27:10.090
For Chinese political soft power.

00:27:10.090 --> 00:27:12.500
We chose a video clip showing people

00:27:12.500 --> 00:27:14.120
in Hong Kong protesting against

00:27:14.120 --> 00:27:15.270
the Chinese government.

00:27:16.235 --> 00:27:18.465
For the Chinese point aid treatment,

00:27:18.465 --> 00:27:23.310
we employed a Chinese state
media promotional video

00:27:23.310 --> 00:27:25.711
on the Belt and Road Initiative.

00:27:25.711 --> 00:27:28.460
All the treatment videos are translated

00:27:28.460 --> 00:27:33.170
and sub-titled according
to the (indistinct)

00:27:33.170 --> 00:27:34.810
respective languages,

00:27:34.810 --> 00:27:37.340
i.e. Korean and Malay.

00:27:37.340 --> 00:27:41.439
Moving on to the key outcomes.

00:27:41.439 --> 00:27:43.550
We focused on three domains

00:27:43.550 --> 00:27:46.290
of international relations
and political economy.

00:27:46.290 --> 00:27:49.363
Our economic consumption variable,

00:27:49.363 --> 00:27:51.990
we asked respondents
whether they preferred to

00:27:51.990 --> 00:27:55.390
purchase Chinese or Japanese
electronic products.

00:27:55.390 --> 00:27:57.010
Respondents were given a choice

00:27:57.010 --> 00:27:58.490
between a Chinese brand

00:27:58.490 --> 00:28:00.940
and a Japanese brand
for three product lines;

00:28:00.940 --> 00:28:05.180
smartphone, TV and home appliance.

00:28:05.180 --> 00:28:06.570
Its brand was randomly drawn

00:28:06.570 --> 00:28:09.568
from the listed brands here on the screen,

00:28:09.568 --> 00:28:12.000
and respondents were asked to indicate

00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:14.290
whether they prefer one or the other

00:28:14.290 --> 00:28:17.300
on the 100 point probability scale.

00:28:17.300 --> 00:28:20.010
We randomized the pair and
the ordering of brands,

00:28:20.010 --> 00:28:22.583
and also the order of the
three electronic items.

00:28:23.580 --> 00:28:25.520
We later rescaled the responses,

00:28:25.520 --> 00:28:27.790
so the 100% represent a purchase

00:28:27.790 --> 00:28:30.790
of the Chinese brand

00:28:30.790 --> 00:28:32.513
over the competing Japanese brand.

00:28:33.470 --> 00:28:36.010
But our two cooperation questions,

00:28:36.010 --> 00:28:37.240
We asked the respondents whether

00:28:37.240 --> 00:28:40.890
they think Korea or
Malaysia should cooperate

00:28:40.890 --> 00:28:42.890
more with Japan and China

00:28:42.890 --> 00:28:45.238
on trade, war, security matters.

00:28:45.238 --> 00:28:48.760
The responses were measured
on a seven point Likert scale,

00:28:48.760 --> 00:28:50.350
running from "strongly agree"

00:28:50.350 --> 00:28:52.487
to "strongly disagree."

00:28:53.365 --> 00:28:57.040
And moving on to kind of mechanism tests.

00:28:57.040 --> 00:28:58.900
In addition to the key outcomes

00:28:58.900 --> 00:29:00.440
that we just rendered,

00:29:00.440 --> 00:29:03.090
we also measured potential outcomes...

00:29:03.090 --> 00:29:05.231
Sorry, potential mechanisms.

00:29:05.231 --> 00:29:08.020
First, we asked respondents how cool

00:29:08.020 --> 00:29:10.410
they think Japan and China is.

00:29:10.410 --> 00:29:12.480
The responses on this
statement were measured

00:29:12.480 --> 00:29:14.670
on a five point scale.

00:29:14.670 --> 00:29:16.360
And second, we provided them

00:29:16.360 --> 00:29:18.080
with an 11 point scale

00:29:18.080 --> 00:29:20.360
where they indicate their emotions

00:29:20.360 --> 00:29:22.931
toward Japan and China, respectively.

00:29:22.931 --> 00:29:27.650
Third, we gather three
types of generalized trust.

00:29:27.650 --> 00:29:30.800
Be they a favorability
rating of Japan and China,

00:29:30.800 --> 00:29:32.320
political trust for the Japanese

00:29:32.320 --> 00:29:34.290
and Chinese governments,

00:29:34.290 --> 00:29:35.810
and economic trust for Japanese

00:29:35.810 --> 00:29:37.220
and Chinese product,

00:29:37.220 --> 00:29:39.620
all of each who are
measured on 100 point scale.

00:29:40.810 --> 00:29:43.723
Our estimation strategy is
the difference-in-means,

00:29:44.577 --> 00:29:47.690
and it's been reported that
pandemic replication surveys are

00:29:47.690 --> 00:29:50.230
comparable to pre-pandemic surveys

00:29:50.230 --> 00:29:52.830
but with some smaller effect sizes.

00:29:52.830 --> 00:29:55.940
Since our surveys were
conducted during COVID-19,

00:29:55.940 --> 00:29:58.210
the estimates of our
(indistinct) effects may be

00:29:58.210 --> 00:29:59.310
somewhat conservative.

00:30:00.187 --> 00:30:02.467
As part of the main result,

00:30:02.467 --> 00:30:05.206
the number one take away is that

00:30:05.206 --> 00:30:09.010
soft power promotion can backfire

00:30:09.010 --> 00:30:12.230
if the bilateral
relations are politicized.

00:30:12.230 --> 00:30:13.310
To support this figure,

00:30:13.310 --> 00:30:15.020
we combined all outcome variables

00:30:15.020 --> 00:30:17.440
into a single dimensional index

00:30:17.440 --> 00:30:19.460
for each soft power sender.

00:30:19.460 --> 00:30:20.900
And we also conflated

00:30:20.900 --> 00:30:24.470
all Japanese or Chinese
treatments for each country.

00:30:24.470 --> 00:30:25.700
We found a large backlash

00:30:25.700 --> 00:30:29.050
against the Japanese soft
power message in South Korea,

00:30:29.050 --> 00:30:31.720
while the Chinese power message featured

00:30:31.720 --> 00:30:34.384
a positive impact in Malaysia.

00:30:34.384 --> 00:30:38.090
And then, we examine the effect

00:30:38.090 --> 00:30:42.800
of each treatment on all
the major outcome questions,

00:30:42.800 --> 00:30:46.903
using the same same strategy
to aggregate the questions.

00:30:47.800 --> 00:30:49.670
Here, we observed some
hierarchical structure

00:30:49.670 --> 00:30:51.747
of soft power sources.

00:30:51.747 --> 00:30:53.610
For example, in Malaysia,

00:30:53.610 --> 00:30:55.390
advertising foreign aid

00:30:55.390 --> 00:30:58.100
significantly moves respondents' attitudes

00:30:58.100 --> 00:30:59.923
in a positive direction.

00:30:59.923 --> 00:31:02.330
And it seems cultural
promoting efforts can

00:31:02.330 --> 00:31:04.313
also payoff in Malaysia.

00:31:05.390 --> 00:31:08.170
However, media exposure
to protest is found

00:31:08.170 --> 00:31:09.470
to have no effect

00:31:09.470 --> 00:31:11.880
or elicit a significant backlash

00:31:11.880 --> 00:31:13.230
for the Japanese treatment.

00:31:14.320 --> 00:31:16.410
In South Korea, we found a backlash

00:31:16.410 --> 00:31:18.680
against all Japanese soft power treatments

00:31:18.680 --> 00:31:21.730
and the Chinese protest treatment.

00:31:21.730 --> 00:31:24.260
We think that the protest may have caused

00:31:24.260 --> 00:31:26.430
some respondents to
update their prior beliefs

00:31:26.430 --> 00:31:28.600
about the the trustworthiness

00:31:28.600 --> 00:31:29.950
of the Japanese government.

00:31:30.795 --> 00:31:34.340
Moving on, we also
investigated the mechanisms

00:31:34.340 --> 00:31:37.570
in which our treatments moved
respondent's perceptions

00:31:37.570 --> 00:31:39.020
about the soft power senders.

00:31:39.970 --> 00:31:42.116
The most notable pattern here is that

00:31:42.116 --> 00:31:45.830
all treatments cause strong
negative emotional reactions

00:31:45.830 --> 00:31:48.783
among South Korea respondents
vis-a-vis Japan and China.

00:31:49.780 --> 00:31:53.970
In Malaysia, we observed
strong negative reactions

00:31:53.970 --> 00:31:56.109
to both Japanese and Chinese protest,

00:31:56.109 --> 00:31:59.050
while the Japanese
cultural treatment induced

00:31:59.050 --> 00:32:00.183
some positive impact.

00:32:01.030 --> 00:32:03.480
Interestingly, we observed
the positive impact

00:32:03.480 --> 00:32:04.830
of Chinese aid

00:32:04.830 --> 00:32:05.900
and the negative impact

00:32:05.900 --> 00:32:10.410
of Japanese protests on
Malaysian respondent's perception

00:32:10.410 --> 00:32:14.610
about the two soft power
senders trustworthiness

00:32:14.610 --> 00:32:17.238
and cool Japan or cool China.

00:32:17.238 --> 00:32:19.240
We can interpret this in line

00:32:19.240 --> 00:32:21.230
with our finding in the paper,

00:32:21.230 --> 00:32:23.970
that the Chinese aid to video works

00:32:23.970 --> 00:32:25.600
for Malaysian respondents

00:32:25.600 --> 00:32:27.840
on security and trade cooperation,

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:31.280
and another finding that
Japanese protest video backfires,

00:32:31.280 --> 00:32:33.760
making Malaysian
respondents dislike security

00:32:33.760 --> 00:32:35.443
and trade cooperation with Japan.

00:32:36.312 --> 00:32:38.780
So in sum, emotion seems to drive

00:32:38.780 --> 00:32:41.840
the backlash we observe
in a politicized setting,

00:32:41.840 --> 00:32:43.285
like in South Korea,

00:32:43.285 --> 00:32:45.960
and generalized trust and coolness matter

00:32:45.960 --> 00:32:48.210
for security and trade cooperation

00:32:48.210 --> 00:32:49.730
in a depoliticized setting,

00:32:49.730 --> 00:32:50.633
like in Malaysia.

00:32:51.550 --> 00:32:53.100
With that, over to you, Hikaru.

00:32:55.956 --> 00:32:58.690
- Great, so for future research,

00:32:58.690 --> 00:33:01.323
there's two avenues that
we'd like to highlight.

00:33:02.240 --> 00:33:06.668
First, one of the three
soft power resources,

00:33:06.668 --> 00:33:08.450
political values.

00:33:08.450 --> 00:33:13.450
So we found that we would
like to unpack this further.

00:33:14.020 --> 00:33:15.640
Our hypothesis was that

00:33:15.640 --> 00:33:17.640
the protests will show freedom,

00:33:17.640 --> 00:33:20.230
but perhaps this was our
political science minds working

00:33:20.230 --> 00:33:21.680
too readily.

00:33:21.680 --> 00:33:24.160
The inherent kind of
paradox of a protest is

00:33:24.160 --> 00:33:27.080
that when you show someone protesting

00:33:27.080 --> 00:33:28.280
against the government,

00:33:28.280 --> 00:33:31.100
that definitionally means
that the government is doing

00:33:31.100 --> 00:33:32.840
something that's not matching the will

00:33:32.840 --> 00:33:34.120
of certain people.

00:33:34.120 --> 00:33:36.690
So it might look like a vision

00:33:36.690 --> 00:33:38.183
of conflict and disorder.

00:33:40.080 --> 00:33:41.430
One way to push forward is

00:33:41.430 --> 00:33:43.820
to show a retrospective narrative of Rome

00:33:43.820 --> 00:33:45.760
when citizens protested

00:33:45.760 --> 00:33:48.053
and the government changed in response.

00:33:49.225 --> 00:33:51.800
And there are also other dimensions

00:33:51.800 --> 00:33:53.730
of political values to explore.

00:33:53.730 --> 00:33:56.970
We might have been too
Western liberal democratic

00:33:56.970 --> 00:33:59.110
in our conception of political values.

00:33:59.110 --> 00:34:00.380
There are many other dimensions,

00:34:00.380 --> 00:34:03.283
like economic performance,
democratic process,

00:34:04.270 --> 00:34:06.011
popular responsiveness,

00:34:06.011 --> 00:34:09.050
that can show attractive
institutional features

00:34:09.050 --> 00:34:10.203
in different forms.

00:34:12.060 --> 00:34:15.080
Secondly, we are left with a question

00:34:15.080 --> 00:34:17.410
of long run effects of soft power.

00:34:17.410 --> 00:34:19.230
We realize that our design is

00:34:19.230 --> 00:34:20.690
a small amount of exposure,

00:34:20.690 --> 00:34:22.080
although similar to government

00:34:22.080 --> 00:34:24.160
public diplomacy efforts perhaps,

00:34:24.160 --> 00:34:26.563
and we can't manipulate how much country

00:34:26.563 --> 00:34:29.100
a soft power has at the baseline.

00:34:29.100 --> 00:34:32.230
We can try to understand long run change

00:34:32.230 --> 00:34:33.620
in soft power, however,

00:34:33.620 --> 00:34:37.270
using panel surveys and
field experiments over time.

00:34:37.270 --> 00:34:40.163
And some examples,

00:34:41.879 --> 00:34:44.520
such as an encouragement
design in the field.

00:34:44.520 --> 00:34:47.520
We could have people subscribed

00:34:47.520 --> 00:34:50.330
to one year subscriptions
of randomly Chinese

00:34:50.330 --> 00:34:52.000
or Japanese media, for instance,

00:34:52.000 --> 00:34:53.740
and see how the slowly changes

00:34:53.740 --> 00:34:56.493
their long-term perceptions and so on.

00:35:00.440 --> 00:35:02.530
In summary, in this paper,

00:35:02.530 --> 00:35:05.520
we propose a logic of
soft power competition

00:35:05.520 --> 00:35:07.810
and test it through survey experiments.

00:35:07.810 --> 00:35:09.710
We hypothesize that the efficacy

00:35:09.710 --> 00:35:11.800
of soft power is limited

00:35:11.800 --> 00:35:13.720
by the politicization of relations

00:35:13.720 --> 00:35:16.458
between the sender and target countries.

00:35:16.458 --> 00:35:20.670
We find that domestic politics does shape

00:35:20.670 --> 00:35:22.200
soft power effects.

00:35:22.200 --> 00:35:24.810
When the bilateral
relationships are politicized,

00:35:24.810 --> 00:35:27.333
soft power efforts can backfire.

00:35:27.333 --> 00:35:30.050
And within soft power resources,

00:35:30.050 --> 00:35:31.830
foreign aid is an easy avenue

00:35:31.830 --> 00:35:34.019
to willing hearts and minds.

00:35:34.019 --> 00:35:36.563
And so, we find that content matters.

00:35:37.713 --> 00:35:40.970
And we also, for next steps,

00:35:40.970 --> 00:35:44.910
find that more research
on this more institutional

00:35:44.910 --> 00:35:48.353
and more nuanced political
values dimension is needed.

00:35:50.710 --> 00:35:51.853
Thank you very much.

00:35:53.568 --> 00:35:55.330
- Great, thank you.

00:35:55.330 --> 00:35:57.718
Thanks so much to this great team

00:35:57.718 --> 00:35:58.870
that I'm a member of

00:35:58.870 --> 00:36:00.960
for this great presentation.

00:36:00.960 --> 00:36:05.302
Now, we have a set of discussion starters

00:36:05.302 --> 00:36:08.150
to get the conversation rolling.

00:36:08.150 --> 00:36:10.770
And please also feel free to continue

00:36:10.770 --> 00:36:13.820
to use the chat function.

00:36:13.820 --> 00:36:16.602
And for attendees,

00:36:16.602 --> 00:36:21.353
you're also welcome to use
the Q&amp;A function as well.

00:36:21.353 --> 00:36:25.731
So first up, we have Professor
Joe Nye from Harvard,

00:36:25.731 --> 00:36:29.710
giving us comments on this paper.

00:36:29.710 --> 00:36:31.230
Thank you.

00:36:31.230 --> 00:36:35.640
- Well, I read this
paper with great interest

00:36:35.640 --> 00:36:40.456
and regard it as a important
contribution empirically,

00:36:40.456 --> 00:36:43.880
which has advanced how we think

00:36:43.880 --> 00:36:44.713
about soft power.

00:36:44.713 --> 00:36:49.160
By using these video
clips and survey research,

00:36:49.160 --> 00:36:53.460
you provided empirical evidence

00:36:53.460 --> 00:36:56.655
from certain things that I had stated

00:36:56.655 --> 00:36:58.430
in general terms

00:36:58.430 --> 00:37:00.295
but never proven.

00:37:00.295 --> 00:37:05.295
I've often said that soft
power is distinguished

00:37:05.740 --> 00:37:07.153
from other types of power in the sense

00:37:07.153 --> 00:37:09.973
that it depends on the
eye of the beholder.

00:37:11.224 --> 00:37:13.340
And I sometimes use the anecdote,

00:37:13.340 --> 00:37:15.520
or a little example,

00:37:15.520 --> 00:37:19.320
that if I want to steal
your money with hard power,

00:37:19.320 --> 00:37:21.285
I pull out a gun, shoot you

00:37:21.285 --> 00:37:22.760
and take your wallet.

00:37:22.760 --> 00:37:24.850
It doesn't matter what you think.

00:37:24.850 --> 00:37:26.410
But if I wanted to steal your money

00:37:26.410 --> 00:37:28.596
by convincing you I'm a guru

00:37:28.596 --> 00:37:31.434
and that you should give it to me,

00:37:31.434 --> 00:37:36.180
it all depends on what you think.

00:37:36.180 --> 00:37:39.620
And so, essentially what
this paper has done is taken

00:37:39.620 --> 00:37:42.157
that question and said, "All right.

00:37:42.157 --> 00:37:44.417
"How do we see how people think

00:37:44.417 --> 00:37:46.657
"and how does it vary
with the different types

00:37:46.657 --> 00:37:48.157
"of soft power resources

00:37:48.157 --> 00:37:49.770
"in the different contexts?"

00:37:49.770 --> 00:37:53.130
So I think it's an important paper,

00:37:53.130 --> 00:37:57.380
which advances (indistinct)

00:38:00.680 --> 00:38:03.480
for something which previously was made

00:38:03.480 --> 00:38:04.743
without any evidence.

00:38:06.510 --> 00:38:09.500
I should say that the...

00:38:10.730 --> 00:38:11.640
I'll tell you,

00:38:11.640 --> 00:38:14.210
this question of which
types of resources have

00:38:14.210 --> 00:38:17.400
which types of effects are intriguing.

00:38:17.400 --> 00:38:19.197
I'll give you a brief anecdote

00:38:19.197 --> 00:38:22.630
to illustrate this related to China.

00:38:22.630 --> 00:38:24.190
I was asked to give a lecture

00:38:24.190 --> 00:38:29.190
on soft power at the School of
Marxism at Peking University,

00:38:31.344 --> 00:38:36.050
in China over a half dozen years.

00:38:36.050 --> 00:38:38.830
And after I spoke to an audience

00:38:38.830 --> 00:38:43.830
of 1,500 students and the
Dean had introduced me,

00:38:44.239 --> 00:38:47.000
everything was going swimmingly,

00:38:47.000 --> 00:38:49.830
except I said something about Ai Weiwei,

00:38:49.830 --> 00:38:53.160
and also about Liu Xiaobo.

00:38:53.160 --> 00:38:56.240
And at the end, when the Dean came up

00:38:56.240 --> 00:38:58.150
from the platform, again, to thank me,

00:38:58.150 --> 00:39:01.877
he said, "Oh, it's so great
that Professor Nye has come

00:39:01.877 --> 00:39:02.950
"and spoken to us."

00:39:02.950 --> 00:39:05.410
And then my interpreter
stopped interpreting,

00:39:05.410 --> 00:39:06.570
and he kept talking

00:39:06.570 --> 00:39:08.640
and then, eventually,

00:39:08.640 --> 00:39:12.360
it ended up with his
thanking me again profusely.

00:39:12.360 --> 00:39:14.340
And later, I asked a friend of mine,

00:39:14.340 --> 00:39:17.727
who is Mandarin speaking
who was in the audience,

00:39:17.727 --> 00:39:19.840
"What was that all about?"

00:39:19.840 --> 00:39:24.241
He said, "What he did
was tell the students

00:39:24.241 --> 00:39:26.937
"that Professor Nye may
have been the person

00:39:26.937 --> 00:39:29.617
"who coined the term "soft power,"

00:39:29.617 --> 00:39:33.097
"but he treated it too
much in political terms

00:39:33.097 --> 00:39:35.773
"and not enough in cultural terms.

00:39:38.077 --> 00:39:39.727
"And here in China,

00:39:39.727 --> 00:39:43.060
"we think of it as culture, not politics."

00:39:43.060 --> 00:39:46.330
So that's an anecdotal illustration

00:39:46.330 --> 00:39:49.510
of the difference between
the three types of resources

00:39:49.510 --> 00:39:51.160
and how it works out politically.

00:39:52.010 --> 00:39:55.010
I think the interesting question for me,

00:39:55.010 --> 00:39:59.180
you've now demonstrated that
priors matter empirically,

00:39:59.180 --> 00:40:00.740
but I think it would
be interesting to know,

00:40:00.740 --> 00:40:02.670
what are the different sources

00:40:02.670 --> 00:40:04.403
of domestic politicization?

00:40:06.030 --> 00:40:09.120
And if you're trying to
translate this to policy,

00:40:09.120 --> 00:40:12.620
what are the things that a country can do

00:40:12.620 --> 00:40:14.203
in its public diplomacy,

00:40:15.080 --> 00:40:19.180
given the politicization
that may or may not occur

00:40:19.180 --> 00:40:21.150
in another country?

00:40:21.150 --> 00:40:26.110
For example, how much of the
domestic politicization is

00:40:28.300 --> 00:40:29.810
because of leaders,

00:40:29.810 --> 00:40:34.130
which the paper refers to,
leaders politicizing it?

00:40:34.130 --> 00:40:36.780
But sometimes, it may be, in fact,

00:40:36.780 --> 00:40:38.563
historical and structural.

00:40:39.420 --> 00:40:43.255
I mean, the Korean
attitudes to Japan persisted

00:40:43.255 --> 00:40:47.193
right through a conservative
Korean government

00:40:47.193 --> 00:40:50.830
and a liberal or progressive
Korean government.

00:40:50.830 --> 00:40:53.280
And they're now bedeviling the efforts

00:40:53.280 --> 00:40:54.470
with the Americans are making

00:40:54.470 --> 00:40:56.520
to try to get Korea and Japan

00:40:56.520 --> 00:41:01.520
to cooperate on the issue of
sharing intelligence relating

00:41:02.640 --> 00:41:03.760
to North Korea.

00:41:03.760 --> 00:41:06.930
So China, in other words,

00:41:06.930 --> 00:41:09.240
it may be that the politicization

00:41:09.240 --> 00:41:13.620
in some cases is
historical and structural.

00:41:13.620 --> 00:41:16.330
And then, the interesting question is,

00:41:16.330 --> 00:41:20.210
to what extent, if the Japanese
government, for example,

00:41:20.210 --> 00:41:23.850
wants to exercise public diplomacy

00:41:23.850 --> 00:41:26.110
and to use its soft power,

00:41:26.110 --> 00:41:28.140
what can it do?

00:41:28.140 --> 00:41:30.590
And I didn't get an answer to that

00:41:30.590 --> 00:41:31.423
from the paper.

00:41:31.423 --> 00:41:33.580
I did get an answer of how it varied

00:41:33.580 --> 00:41:35.970
by the different resources,

00:41:35.970 --> 00:41:37.210
but I didn't actually...

00:41:37.210 --> 00:41:38.912
It'd be nice to have another twist

00:41:38.912 --> 00:41:40.760
of the screw on this,

00:41:40.760 --> 00:41:42.550
one step further, to know,

00:41:42.550 --> 00:41:43.970
all right, what could that mean

00:41:43.970 --> 00:41:47.800
for actual practitioners
of public diplomacy?

00:41:47.800 --> 00:41:48.750
There are...

00:41:48.750 --> 00:41:50.350
Also, it would be interesting

00:41:50.350 --> 00:41:52.960
to know something about time series.

00:41:52.960 --> 00:41:56.270
I mean, this is churlish to complain

00:41:56.270 --> 00:41:58.980
that you don't have time series,

00:41:58.980 --> 00:42:03.119
but it would be interesting to know

00:42:03.119 --> 00:42:05.540
what happens when events occur

00:42:06.890 --> 00:42:07.960
and what happens over time?

00:42:07.960 --> 00:42:10.253
How do these variables change?

00:42:11.422 --> 00:42:15.061
I noticed that you quoted
Naima Green-Riley's

00:42:15.061 --> 00:42:19.162
very good thesis on soft power,

00:42:19.162 --> 00:42:24.162
showing that American
students who had taken courses

00:42:28.260 --> 00:42:32.690
through Confucius Institute
were not more pro-Chinese,

00:42:32.690 --> 00:42:34.083
but less so.

00:42:34.083 --> 00:42:35.330
It'd be interesting to know

00:42:35.330 --> 00:42:36.553
if one were to look...

00:42:36.553 --> 00:42:37.874
I don't know, Naima,

00:42:37.874 --> 00:42:39.430
whether you have the data

00:42:39.430 --> 00:42:40.263
on this or not.

00:42:40.263 --> 00:42:41.790
It'd be interesting to know

00:42:41.790 --> 00:42:43.730
whether that would have
been true, let's say,

00:42:43.730 --> 00:42:45.190
six or seven years ago

00:42:45.190 --> 00:42:48.160
before you had the turn in the attitude

00:42:48.160 --> 00:42:51.910
of Americans relating to China or not.

00:42:51.910 --> 00:42:54.790
But no, I think, if for
future empirical studies,

00:42:54.790 --> 00:42:57.550
if we could get more time series,

00:42:57.550 --> 00:42:58.870
that would help a lot.

00:42:58.870 --> 00:43:00.160
And there's also, finally,

00:43:00.160 --> 00:43:05.160
the question of what
policies governments make

00:43:06.140 --> 00:43:11.140
which can bring on the
backlash that you described.

00:43:12.210 --> 00:43:15.700
I'm struck, having recently been involved

00:43:15.700 --> 00:43:17.360
in discussions with Indians,

00:43:17.360 --> 00:43:21.055
who the aftermath of the
recent meeting of this Quad

00:43:21.055 --> 00:43:22.963
that occurred a couple of weeks ago,

00:43:23.820 --> 00:43:26.820
Indian said that it was intriguing that

00:43:27.661 --> 00:43:30.630
when China started exercising

00:43:30.630 --> 00:43:32.800
its Wolf-Warrior diplomacy,

00:43:32.800 --> 00:43:36.490
which was obviously based on
domestic opinion in China,

00:43:36.490 --> 00:43:38.520
it was for domestic consumption,

00:43:38.520 --> 00:43:41.600
that had a terrible effect in India.

00:43:41.600 --> 00:43:44.490
Not only the killing of Indian troops

00:43:44.490 --> 00:43:45.770
on the border,

00:43:45.770 --> 00:43:47.470
but the aftermath of that,

00:43:47.470 --> 00:43:50.100
in which you had Chinese nationalism

00:43:50.100 --> 00:43:53.703
stimulating Indian nationalist responses,

00:43:55.112 --> 00:43:57.430
which led, incidentally, to the banning

00:43:57.430 --> 00:43:59.363
of Tik Tok in India.

00:43:59.363 --> 00:44:02.028
So it'd be interesting to know,

00:44:02.028 --> 00:44:05.870
are there certain steps
that governments take

00:44:05.870 --> 00:44:10.870
which can damage or severely
damage it's soft power

00:44:12.750 --> 00:44:15.700
in another country in
which that can be avoided?

00:44:15.700 --> 00:44:17.670
Still, I love the paper.

00:44:17.670 --> 00:44:19.370
I thought it was great to have

00:44:19.370 --> 00:44:22.253
this empirical research.

00:44:24.150 --> 00:44:27.870
But it just made me thirst for more.

00:44:27.870 --> 00:44:29.880
More on time series,

00:44:29.880 --> 00:44:32.200
more at a wider of countries.

00:44:32.200 --> 00:44:35.450
But anyway, as a major contribution

00:44:35.450 --> 00:44:37.830
to getting empirical research

00:44:37.830 --> 00:44:40.140
upon the effects of soft power,

00:44:40.140 --> 00:44:43.170
my congratulations to the to the authors,

00:44:43.170 --> 00:44:46.710
all seven of them.

00:44:46.710 --> 00:44:49.354
- Thank you, thank you
so much, Professor Nye.

00:44:49.354 --> 00:44:50.570
(indistinct) I call for more

00:44:50.570 --> 00:44:52.070
more research on this important topic

00:44:52.070 --> 00:44:53.640
among other things.

00:44:53.640 --> 00:44:55.640
Next, we have Professor Maria Repnikova.

00:44:59.730 --> 00:45:01.400
Maria--
- Yes, can you hear me?

00:45:01.400 --> 00:45:02.928
Great.

00:45:02.928 --> 00:45:03.761
Thank you so much for the invitation.

00:45:03.761 --> 00:45:04.594
I also really enjoyed the paper.

00:45:04.594 --> 00:45:05.860
I think it's very rich empirically

00:45:05.860 --> 00:45:07.230
but I also found a lot of

00:45:07.230 --> 00:45:08.550
interesting theoretical insights.

00:45:08.550 --> 00:45:10.660
For me, one of the big takeaways was

00:45:10.660 --> 00:45:14.290
also the breakdown of the
democratic-authoritarian binary

00:45:14.290 --> 00:45:16.300
and analyzing the effects of soft power,

00:45:16.300 --> 00:45:17.720
which I think has been overshadowing

00:45:17.720 --> 00:45:19.270
much of the analysis of soft power,

00:45:19.270 --> 00:45:20.690
especially when it comes to China.

00:45:20.690 --> 00:45:23.240
This idea that because China
is an autocratic country,

00:45:23.240 --> 00:45:25.830
its efforts are dismissed preemptively,

00:45:25.830 --> 00:45:27.380
or at least considered to be ineffective,

00:45:27.380 --> 00:45:28.850
and I think your paper shows that

00:45:28.850 --> 00:45:30.590
the fact that China is an
autocratic country doesn't

00:45:30.590 --> 00:45:31.960
necessarily make it ineffective

00:45:31.960 --> 00:45:32.980
or a failure.

00:45:32.980 --> 00:45:35.600
And in fact, because Japan
is a democratic country,

00:45:35.600 --> 00:45:37.912
it doesn't necessarily make it a success.

00:45:37.912 --> 00:45:39.480
So there's kind of this
interesting complication

00:45:39.480 --> 00:45:41.450
of the democracy-autocracy binary

00:45:41.450 --> 00:45:43.172
in thinking about soft power.

00:45:43.172 --> 00:45:44.412
I think you could highlight that even more

00:45:44.412 --> 00:45:46.720
in your theoretical contributions.

00:45:46.720 --> 00:45:49.180
My comments, some of them also
echo Professor Nye's comment

00:45:49.180 --> 00:45:51.560
about the concept of depoliticization,

00:45:51.560 --> 00:45:53.110
how to think about this further.

00:45:53.110 --> 00:45:55.060
So there are several
points I wanted to make.

00:45:55.060 --> 00:45:58.480
The first one is that
depoliticization, to me,

00:45:58.480 --> 00:46:00.000
echoing this idea of time series,

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:01.914
it appears to be a very dynamic concept.

00:46:01.914 --> 00:46:04.790
So it challenges its operationalization.

00:46:04.790 --> 00:46:05.920
How do we know for sure

00:46:05.920 --> 00:46:07.450
when the relationship is politicized

00:46:07.450 --> 00:46:08.900
versus depoliticized?

00:46:08.900 --> 00:46:10.560
For example, in 2018,

00:46:10.560 --> 00:46:12.500
the Malaysian PM publicly canceled

00:46:12.500 --> 00:46:14.140
a major Chinese infrastructure project,

00:46:14.140 --> 00:46:15.670
creating a rupture in the relationship

00:46:15.670 --> 00:46:16.820
but then a year later.

00:46:16.820 --> 00:46:20.110
he takes a U-turn in resuming
the relationship with China.

00:46:20.110 --> 00:46:21.510
So how does this concept

00:46:21.510 --> 00:46:23.180
that comes with such dynamic shifts

00:46:23.180 --> 00:46:25.730
and what specific forms
of behavior over time

00:46:25.730 --> 00:46:28.460
amount to this idea of
politicization of ties?

00:46:28.460 --> 00:46:31.100
So how do we know when
they're politicized enough

00:46:31.100 --> 00:46:31.933
or not enough?

00:46:31.933 --> 00:46:33.977
I think it's a very fluid concept in a way

00:46:33.977 --> 00:46:35.200
and it quickly changes,

00:46:35.200 --> 00:46:36.880
so maybe it's about watching it

00:46:36.880 --> 00:46:37.870
or observing it over time,

00:46:37.870 --> 00:46:41.370
or maybe it's about adding
more alternative variables

00:46:41.370 --> 00:46:43.300
or sub-variables that
would help us explain

00:46:43.300 --> 00:46:44.650
or understand this concept.

00:46:45.500 --> 00:46:47.589
The second point in
relation to this concept,

00:46:47.589 --> 00:46:49.022
I think, this echoes this idea of

00:46:49.022 --> 00:46:51.790
this notion that politicization
comes from leaders.

00:46:51.790 --> 00:46:53.720
The way you define it is very top down.

00:46:53.720 --> 00:46:55.660
Basically, the government defines

00:46:55.660 --> 00:46:58.640
or forms or shapes the
opinions of the society

00:46:58.640 --> 00:47:00.890
when it comes to these various countries.

00:47:00.890 --> 00:47:02.640
But in reality, seems
that this relationship is

00:47:02.640 --> 00:47:03.890
often quite interactive, right?

00:47:03.890 --> 00:47:05.290
The society itself often pushes

00:47:05.290 --> 00:47:08.805
the government to frame or
implement certain policies

00:47:08.805 --> 00:47:10.640
that are much more politicized.

00:47:10.640 --> 00:47:11.473
And we see this, of course,

00:47:11.473 --> 00:47:12.306
in the US vis-a-vis China.

00:47:12.306 --> 00:47:14.449
I think it's also notable
in the cases you study.

00:47:14.449 --> 00:47:16.610
But also, in many other contexts,

00:47:16.610 --> 00:47:17.680
it seems that the public opinion

00:47:17.680 --> 00:47:20.000
actually shapes this
politicization efforts

00:47:20.000 --> 00:47:20.833
from the top.

00:47:20.833 --> 00:47:22.520
And then, moreover, we also can see that

00:47:22.520 --> 00:47:25.380
at times, politicization
of societal attitudes is

00:47:25.380 --> 00:47:26.213
not the same thing

00:47:26.213 --> 00:47:28.630
or in contradiction to official policies.

00:47:28.630 --> 00:47:30.570
So I see this a lot in
my research in Africa

00:47:30.570 --> 00:47:31.950
where, with regards to China,

00:47:31.950 --> 00:47:35.240
we see often very
"depoliticized" sort of attitudes

00:47:35.240 --> 00:47:36.140
from the state

00:47:36.140 --> 00:47:37.580
but very politicized attitudes

00:47:37.580 --> 00:47:38.830
on a societal level,

00:47:38.830 --> 00:47:41.360
where the public is very critical of China

00:47:41.360 --> 00:47:43.320
and has preconceived views about China,

00:47:43.320 --> 00:47:44.930
based on its economic presence,

00:47:44.930 --> 00:47:47.280
but also various rumors and assumptions

00:47:47.280 --> 00:47:49.500
about its corrupt practices, interference,

00:47:49.500 --> 00:47:51.280
Chinese community taking
over and so forth.

00:47:51.280 --> 00:47:53.250
So in a sense, there's
these predetermined views

00:47:53.250 --> 00:47:56.310
that can coexist with
official depoliticization.

00:47:56.310 --> 00:47:58.010
So I think those two can be contradictory.

00:47:58.010 --> 00:48:00.070
So there's more, I think,
to think about there,

00:48:00.070 --> 00:48:01.370
of how you think about this concept?

00:48:01.370 --> 00:48:02.590
Top-down versus bottom-up.

00:48:02.590 --> 00:48:04.260
Interactive, contradictory,

00:48:04.260 --> 00:48:06.120
it's a very interesting idea.

00:48:06.120 --> 00:48:08.020
And thirdly, I think depoliticization

00:48:08.020 --> 00:48:09.640
almost presumes, in your idea,

00:48:09.640 --> 00:48:11.410
in your paper, neutrality.

00:48:11.410 --> 00:48:13.020
The lack of negative framing.

00:48:13.020 --> 00:48:14.520
But in reality, politicization

00:48:14.520 --> 00:48:15.840
in the other direction is also,

00:48:15.840 --> 00:48:16.770
I think, quite significant.

00:48:16.770 --> 00:48:18.770
The pro-China narratives

00:48:18.770 --> 00:48:20.220
or pro-Japan narratives.

00:48:20.220 --> 00:48:21.460
In the case of Malaysia, for instance,

00:48:21.460 --> 00:48:23.040
there's been so much politicization

00:48:23.040 --> 00:48:26.257
in the case of pro-China
attitudes, in recent years,

00:48:26.257 --> 00:48:28.070
through various state media articles.

00:48:28.070 --> 00:48:28.903
Just a quick search,

00:48:28.903 --> 00:48:29.960
I found so many articles that are

00:48:29.960 --> 00:48:31.480
very positive about China

00:48:31.480 --> 00:48:32.610
in Malaysian press.

00:48:32.610 --> 00:48:33.770
That, to me, signals that there is

00:48:33.770 --> 00:48:36.170
the other direction of politicization,

00:48:36.170 --> 00:48:37.610
which one could call propaganda,

00:48:37.610 --> 00:48:39.220
persuasion and so forth.

00:48:39.220 --> 00:48:41.030
So how does that play into this picture?

00:48:41.030 --> 00:48:42.150
Should we look at politicization

00:48:42.150 --> 00:48:44.174
from the other side as well?

00:48:44.174 --> 00:48:47.110
In addition to this thinking
about politicization,

00:48:47.110 --> 00:48:50.400
there are a couple of other
points I wanted to make.

00:48:50.400 --> 00:48:52.780
The first one is about
practices of soft power.

00:48:52.780 --> 00:48:54.526
So you talk a lot about the effects

00:48:54.526 --> 00:48:57.276
but when it comes to
practices, for instance,

00:48:57.276 --> 00:48:59.300
are there any major distinctions

00:48:59.300 --> 00:49:01.100
in how China and Japan
practice soft power?

00:49:01.100 --> 00:49:02.460
You mentioned the resources

00:49:02.460 --> 00:49:04.430
but do they enact it differently?

00:49:04.430 --> 00:49:05.430
In one part of your paper,

00:49:05.430 --> 00:49:07.610
you note how Japan practices foreign aid

00:49:07.610 --> 00:49:09.590
more through small scale projects,

00:49:09.590 --> 00:49:11.240
as opposed to large scale, visible,

00:49:11.240 --> 00:49:13.980
or what some school is referred
to as "prestige projects,"

00:49:13.980 --> 00:49:15.690
that are implemented by China.

00:49:15.690 --> 00:49:17.560
Another notable difference might be

00:49:17.560 --> 00:49:18.640
there's more state-directed way

00:49:18.640 --> 00:49:20.230
of soft power implementation in China

00:49:20.230 --> 00:49:22.800
versus more kind of bottom
up implementation from Japan.

00:49:22.800 --> 00:49:24.080
And now, there might be the emphasis

00:49:24.080 --> 00:49:25.540
of the key offerings of soft power,

00:49:25.540 --> 00:49:27.790
which I guess is hard to
capture in this paper.

00:49:27.790 --> 00:49:28.623
But in my research,

00:49:28.623 --> 00:49:30.260
I find that China's
approach to soft power is

00:49:30.260 --> 00:49:31.700
distinctively pragmatic, for instance.

00:49:31.700 --> 00:49:33.180
Much less so ideational.

00:49:33.180 --> 00:49:35.320
And precisely because of
its pragmatic approach,

00:49:35.320 --> 00:49:37.490
to prioritize access
and material benefits,

00:49:37.490 --> 00:49:39.440
it appears to be relatively well received

00:49:39.440 --> 00:49:40.799
in parts of Global South.

00:49:40.799 --> 00:49:42.750
So what are the distinctions

00:49:42.750 --> 00:49:44.980
between, I think, Japan
in this case and China,

00:49:44.980 --> 00:49:46.180
when it comes to their practices

00:49:46.180 --> 00:49:47.013
and their offerings,

00:49:47.013 --> 00:49:48.210
not only when it comes to effects?

00:49:48.210 --> 00:49:49.290
I think that will be very interesting

00:49:49.290 --> 00:49:52.250
to at least mention
somewhere in the paper.

00:49:52.250 --> 00:49:53.790
And I think, finally,
while it's impossible

00:49:53.790 --> 00:49:55.760
to, I guess, address any or all

00:49:55.760 --> 00:49:57.640
possible alternative factors or forces

00:49:57.640 --> 00:49:58.730
that could shape the attitudes

00:49:58.730 --> 00:49:59.940
towards soft power,

00:49:59.940 --> 00:50:01.130
in this context, it's important to

00:50:01.130 --> 00:50:03.970
at least consider two complimentary,
I think, explanations.

00:50:03.970 --> 00:50:05.207
One is economic;

00:50:05.207 --> 00:50:08.860
both South Korea and Malaysia have

00:50:08.860 --> 00:50:10.870
very strong trade relations with China

00:50:10.870 --> 00:50:12.100
but less so with Japan,

00:50:12.100 --> 00:50:13.810
so there's an economic
kind of dimension there.

00:50:13.810 --> 00:50:16.060
But also, Malaysia clearly has a much

00:50:16.060 --> 00:50:18.730
stronger dependency on China with the BRI,

00:50:18.730 --> 00:50:20.520
so how does this economic factor feature

00:50:20.520 --> 00:50:22.700
into politicization or the attitudes

00:50:22.700 --> 00:50:25.722
vis-a-vis China and Japan?

00:50:25.722 --> 00:50:28.380
And another complimentary
factor is cultural proximity.

00:50:28.380 --> 00:50:30.910
You mentioned the diasporic factor briefly

00:50:30.910 --> 00:50:32.300
