00:58:56.310 --> 00:58:58.280
behind it to secure supporting voices

00:58:58.280 --> 00:59:00.199
in the United Nations, for instance.

00:59:00.199 --> 00:59:04.040
And so, the more recent
cases, for instance,

00:59:04.040 --> 00:59:05.680
the vaccine diplomacy,

00:59:05.680 --> 00:59:07.040
so-called "vaccine diplomacy,"

00:59:07.040 --> 00:59:09.460
it's part of foreign aid.

00:59:09.460 --> 00:59:11.720
But for instance, Paraguay
was asked by China

00:59:11.720 --> 00:59:14.290
to cut off diplomatic
relations with Taiwan

00:59:14.290 --> 00:59:16.090
in exchange for providing the vaccine,

00:59:16.090 --> 00:59:18.500
so obviously there are
some kind of overlap

00:59:18.500 --> 00:59:21.140
between these kind of seemingly soft power

00:59:21.140 --> 00:59:23.236
and different strategies

00:59:23.236 --> 00:59:25.780
and the hard part,

00:59:25.780 --> 00:59:29.930
which basically coerce some
specific type of actions

00:59:29.930 --> 00:59:32.190
of the recipient of this aid.

00:59:32.190 --> 00:59:34.690
So that's my theoretical question.

00:59:34.690 --> 00:59:37.270
The two methodological questions concerns

00:59:37.270 --> 00:59:38.823
experimental stimuli.

00:59:38.823 --> 00:59:40.990
