01:02:34.100 --> 01:02:36.705
and to the discussion so far.

01:02:36.705 --> 01:02:38.570
You've already been asking questions

01:02:38.570 --> 01:02:41.730
about the details of the
treatments and the data.

01:02:41.730 --> 01:02:44.370
I wanted to ask two big picture questions

01:02:44.370 --> 01:02:46.650
about the punchline of the paper,

01:02:46.650 --> 01:02:47.690
one is theoretical

01:02:47.690 --> 01:02:50.160
and the other is practical.

01:02:50.160 --> 01:02:51.580
So at a theoretical level,

01:02:51.580 --> 01:02:54.247
one way, I think, to read this paper is

01:02:54.247 --> 01:02:56.390
to say that the paper's claiming

01:02:56.390 --> 01:02:59.147
that attempts to promote
soft power will fail

01:02:59.147 --> 01:03:01.780
if the intended audiences are hostile

01:03:01.780 --> 01:03:03.170
to the message.

01:03:03.170 --> 01:03:04.780
Or if you want to flip that around,

01:03:04.780 --> 01:03:05.820
another way of saying is

01:03:05.820 --> 01:03:07.960
that countries are more
likely to be successful

01:03:07.960 --> 01:03:09.750
at promoting soft power

01:03:09.750 --> 01:03:11.424
when they're preaching to the choir

01:03:11.424 --> 01:03:13.460
than when they're not.

01:03:13.460 --> 01:03:14.293
So I wanted...

01:03:14.293 --> 01:03:15.490
So my first question is,

01:03:15.490 --> 01:03:16.800
whether at a theoretical level,

01:03:16.800 --> 01:03:18.730
you think that that's a
fair reading of the paper

01:03:18.730 --> 01:03:19.760
and if so, if you think

01:03:19.760 --> 01:03:22.645
that that's a surprising
theoretical claim.

01:03:22.645 --> 01:03:25.150
My second question is about

01:03:25.150 --> 01:03:28.100
the practical implications
of your research

01:03:28.100 --> 01:03:29.760
for policymakers.

01:03:29.760 --> 01:03:32.130
So another big picture takeaway

01:03:32.130 --> 01:03:33.440
or way to read this paper is

01:03:33.440 --> 01:03:35.480
that efforts to promote soft power are

01:03:35.480 --> 01:03:38.090
most likely to fail in dyads

01:03:38.090 --> 01:03:40.478
where soft power might be most needed.

01:03:40.478 --> 01:03:43.030
And if you think that
that's a fair reading,

01:03:43.030 --> 01:03:45.100
do you think your findings
might be discouraging

01:03:45.100 --> 01:03:48.300
to policymakers who want to use soft power

01:03:48.300 --> 01:03:50.040
as a way to improve relations

01:03:50.040 --> 01:03:52.973
in dyads that might not
otherwise be harmonious?

01:03:56.870 --> 01:03:58.130
- Great, thank you.

01:03:58.130 --> 01:04:00.013
Next, Professor Amy Catalinac.

01:04:01.279 --> 01:04:03.750
- Yeah. I also really enjoyed the paper

01:04:03.750 --> 01:04:05.610
and the presentation.

01:04:05.610 --> 01:04:06.690
It's just fascinating.

01:04:06.690 --> 01:04:08.570
So my comment is jumping off

01:04:08.570 --> 01:04:10.590
something that Mike said.

01:04:10.590 --> 01:04:13.040
So one of your conclusions, I think,

01:04:13.040 --> 01:04:14.390
is that soft power,

01:04:14.390 --> 01:04:17.050
it just doesn't work and
politicized environments

01:04:17.050 --> 01:04:19.280
when the relationship between the sender

01:04:19.280 --> 01:04:21.540
and the target is politicized.

01:04:21.540 --> 01:04:24.630
So that suggests that if that's the case,

01:04:24.630 --> 01:04:27.821
governments shouldn't be
directing soft power resources

01:04:27.821 --> 01:04:30.010
to those countries.

01:04:30.010 --> 01:04:32.140
And I wonder if a really nice compliment

01:04:32.140 --> 01:04:34.200
to your experiment might be

01:04:34.200 --> 01:04:36.840
to look at the composition of governments'

01:04:36.840 --> 01:04:38.140
soft power resources.

01:04:38.140 --> 01:04:41.200
Do we see a bias in
directing those resources

01:04:41.200 --> 01:04:43.900
toward countries where
they are going to work?

01:04:43.900 --> 01:04:45.450
and your theory would lead us

01:04:45.450 --> 01:04:48.380
to expect that governments,
if they're smart,

01:04:48.380 --> 01:04:50.070
should not be spending too much money

01:04:50.070 --> 01:04:51.580
in politicized environments.

01:04:51.580 --> 01:04:53.040
I just wonder if that's the case.

01:04:53.040 --> 01:04:56.380
And also, just to echo what Mike said,

01:04:56.380 --> 01:04:58.070
it is interesting to think about the fact

01:04:58.070 --> 01:04:59.690
that that is sort of where

01:04:59.690 --> 01:05:02.160
soft power should be most directed.

01:05:02.160 --> 01:05:05.660
So it might help us advance the concept

01:05:05.660 --> 01:05:07.347
of soft power to understand

01:05:07.347 --> 01:05:09.454
when and why it's used.

01:05:09.454 --> 01:05:13.347
And I'm in general, just a
fan of observational work,

01:05:13.347 --> 01:05:16.407
especially when it
compliments experiments.

01:05:16.407 --> 01:05:18.100
But thank you for the opportunity

01:05:18.100 --> 01:05:20.433
to read the paper and
see the presentation.

01:05:22.270 --> 01:05:23.397
- Great, thanks so much.

01:05:23.397 --> 01:05:26.773
Next, we have Jennifer Lind
and Benjamin Goldsmith.

01:05:30.120 --> 01:05:31.360
- Hi everyone.

01:05:31.360 --> 01:05:32.780
Lovely to see everybody.

01:05:32.780 --> 01:05:34.428
I hope you're all well.

01:05:34.428 --> 01:05:36.670
I just had a couple of comments on

01:05:36.670 --> 01:05:39.073
this really interesting paper.

01:05:39.073 --> 01:05:42.840
So I don't work in the
soft power realm so much,

01:05:42.840 --> 01:05:45.880
so I would love to see you frame it

01:05:45.880 --> 01:05:47.803
by first establishing,

01:05:49.567 --> 01:05:50.987
"soft power matters.

01:05:50.987 --> 01:05:51.897
"Here's why.

01:05:51.897 --> 01:05:54.410
"Here's the empirical support," right?

01:05:54.410 --> 01:05:56.360
So point is to the studies

01:05:56.360 --> 01:06:00.229
that show that this
actually has this effect.

01:06:00.229 --> 01:06:05.229
Also to say, soft power
comes from many sources,

01:06:05.376 --> 01:06:08.070
but one way that we know

01:06:08.070 --> 01:06:10.270
that countries can get soft power is

01:06:10.270 --> 01:06:12.881
through their policies.

01:06:12.881 --> 01:06:15.550
And here's why we know this,

01:06:15.550 --> 01:06:17.650
here's the empirical support and so on.

01:06:17.650 --> 01:06:20.650
So again, it's establishing

01:06:20.650 --> 01:06:23.460
that soft power is not just something

01:06:23.460 --> 01:06:24.710
that a country is

01:06:24.710 --> 01:06:29.710
or that relates to its
generalized foreign policy

01:06:29.775 --> 01:06:32.180
but that you can...

01:06:32.180 --> 01:06:33.850
I don't want to say by soft power

01:06:33.850 --> 01:06:35.790
but that you can affect it

01:06:35.790 --> 01:06:37.620
through government policies

01:06:37.620 --> 01:06:42.620
to make your country seem more favorably.

01:06:44.310 --> 01:06:47.980
So again, it would be soft power matters,

01:06:47.980 --> 01:06:52.980
soft power comes from both
who you are and what you do,

01:06:53.190 --> 01:06:55.387
but also through deliberate,

01:06:55.387 --> 01:06:58.170
proactive efforts to cultivate it.

01:06:58.170 --> 01:07:00.787
And then, the question
of your paper could be,

01:07:00.787 --> 01:07:03.467
"Okay, so under what conditions will

01:07:03.467 --> 01:07:05.137
"a country be more successful

01:07:05.137 --> 01:07:06.570
"at cultivating it?"

01:07:06.570 --> 01:07:09.643
And I think that that
brings you to your paper.

01:07:10.856 --> 01:07:13.090
I had...

01:07:13.090 --> 01:07:15.500
So again, there's so much to like here

01:07:15.500 --> 01:07:18.270
and I had so many questions,

01:07:18.270 --> 01:07:19.670
but one thing I'll just mention

01:07:19.670 --> 01:07:24.670
that kind of stuck out
was, unsurprisingly,

01:07:26.010 --> 01:07:27.858
on the issue of historical memory.

01:07:27.858 --> 01:07:31.460
The paper creates these three categories

01:07:31.460 --> 01:07:34.708
of culture, politics and foreign policy.

01:07:34.708 --> 01:07:37.970
And you talk about if relations are bad

01:07:37.970 --> 01:07:39.263
in the culture realm,

01:07:40.165 --> 01:07:41.230
then they'll only be aggravated

01:07:41.230 --> 01:07:43.660
by a government's efforts
to build soft power

01:07:43.660 --> 01:07:44.723
in that realm.

01:07:45.941 --> 01:07:47.820
And so, that made me wonder about

01:07:49.466 --> 01:07:51.134
this culture category.

01:07:51.134 --> 01:07:54.550
I guess I'm not sure about the connection

01:07:54.550 --> 01:07:58.534
between Japanese anime and sushi,

01:07:58.534 --> 01:08:02.240
and political apologies, right?

01:08:02.240 --> 01:08:04.923
Which you kind of put it in that category.

01:08:05.820 --> 01:08:07.720
And so, these things just seem

01:08:07.720 --> 01:08:08.790
really different to me.

01:08:08.790 --> 01:08:10.820
Again, the paper says that relations

01:08:10.820 --> 01:08:12.940
between South Korea and
Japan are politicized

01:08:12.940 --> 01:08:15.100
because of culture, historical grievances

01:08:15.100 --> 01:08:17.030
and diplomatic conflicts,

01:08:17.030 --> 01:08:20.905
and a lot of that sounds
like politics to me.

01:08:20.905 --> 01:08:23.870
In fact, I think of the countries

01:08:23.870 --> 01:08:26.925
as actually having really
good cultural relations.

01:08:26.925 --> 01:08:29.480
K-pop and J-pop and all the things

01:08:29.480 --> 01:08:31.716
that they share.

01:08:31.716 --> 01:08:35.063
So again, it's mostly politics,

01:08:35.063 --> 01:08:37.100
political apologies

01:08:37.100 --> 01:08:40.065
and the territorial dispute
over Dokdo Takeshima,

01:08:40.065 --> 01:08:42.673
that seemed to be the root of the problem.

01:08:43.630 --> 01:08:44.760
So again, I was confused

01:08:44.760 --> 01:08:48.340
about putting everything
into that culture bucket.

01:08:48.340 --> 01:08:51.300
I had one reaction in terms of

01:08:51.300 --> 01:08:53.337
at the end of the day, people could say,

01:08:53.337 --> 01:08:55.860
"Okay, these are just
these single case studies."

01:08:55.860 --> 01:08:59.190
You have one neutral, as
you called it, country,

01:08:59.190 --> 01:09:01.570
you had one politicized country,

01:09:01.570 --> 01:09:02.970
and maybe you've just taught us

01:09:02.970 --> 01:09:04.830
something about Malaysia and Japan...

01:09:04.830 --> 01:09:07.741
Oh sorry, Malaysia and Korea.

01:09:07.741 --> 01:09:10.310
Is there something
simple that you could do

01:09:10.310 --> 01:09:14.210
to bolster yourselves
against that kind of charge?

01:09:14.210 --> 01:09:16.900
So maybe, obviously, not running

01:09:16.900 --> 01:09:18.247
this whole thing again

01:09:18.247 --> 01:09:19.660
and in these other countries

01:09:19.660 --> 01:09:24.660
but like mechanical Turk
in some smaller study,

01:09:25.660 --> 01:09:28.070
where you could just kick the tires of it?

01:09:28.070 --> 01:09:32.870
And in other countries that...

01:09:32.870 --> 01:09:37.360
Singapore and Philippines,

01:09:37.360 --> 01:09:41.600
Australia, European countries
or something like that.

01:09:41.600 --> 01:09:43.260
if there would be something simple

01:09:43.260 --> 01:09:46.130
that you could do in three or four,

01:09:46.130 --> 01:09:48.040
four or five other countries

01:09:48.040 --> 01:09:49.057
that people would agree,

01:09:49.057 --> 01:09:51.210
"Yeah, they're quite neutral."

01:09:51.210 --> 01:09:53.090
And if you, you found actually

01:09:53.090 --> 01:09:54.410
quite similar findings,

01:09:54.410 --> 01:09:56.050
I think that would bolster

01:09:56.050 --> 01:09:57.600
the credibility of your answer.

01:10:01.747 --> 01:10:03.977
- Yeah, and I guess I'm next.

01:10:03.977 --> 01:10:06.330
So just a couple of comments.

01:10:06.330 --> 01:10:07.580
Yeah, thanks very much.

01:10:07.580 --> 01:10:09.430
I also really, really liked the paper

01:10:09.430 --> 01:10:13.010
and find it fascinating

01:10:13.010 --> 01:10:17.526
in providing a lot of important insights.

01:10:17.526 --> 01:10:20.497
A couple of comments.

01:10:20.497 --> 01:10:24.221
One reflects a comment I have in the chat.

01:10:24.221 --> 01:10:29.190
I especially like the focus on competition

01:10:29.190 --> 01:10:32.245
in the theoretical
discussion and the framing.

01:10:32.245 --> 01:10:36.560
But I also think that's
really underdeveloped

01:10:36.560 --> 01:10:38.571
in the theory especially.

01:10:38.571 --> 01:10:41.190
It's mentioned and the theory is presented

01:10:41.190 --> 01:10:43.699
as a theory of soft power competition.

01:10:43.699 --> 01:10:47.220
I would be pretty excited to see

01:10:47.220 --> 01:10:50.240
a nicely developed theory, for example,

01:10:50.240 --> 01:10:52.583
when two states intend to compete.

01:10:55.603 --> 01:10:57.280
You mentioned China in the US

01:10:57.280 --> 01:10:58.955
as the obvious example.

01:10:58.955 --> 01:11:01.240
Think of Africa or South America,

01:11:01.240 --> 01:11:02.520
and of course, Asia as well,

01:11:02.520 --> 01:11:05.900
but Japan and China are clearly competing

01:11:05.900 --> 01:11:07.970
and intend to compete, in my view,

01:11:07.970 --> 01:11:11.543
in Asia in the soft power realm.

01:11:13.310 --> 01:11:14.550
But as the theory...

01:11:14.550 --> 01:11:16.060
As its spelled out in most parts

01:11:16.060 --> 01:11:18.463
of this study are are bilateral

01:11:18.463 --> 01:11:21.211
in the way they're set up.

01:11:21.211 --> 01:11:26.211
And the second comment is about the

01:11:30.739 --> 01:11:32.320
the role of culture.

01:11:32.320 --> 01:11:36.384
So I think there's an issue with

01:11:36.384 --> 01:11:39.320
the way culture is discussed.

01:11:39.320 --> 01:11:40.670
I think it needs to be defined

01:11:40.670 --> 01:11:43.030
much more clearly.

01:11:43.030 --> 01:11:44.893
Anything from being cool

01:11:44.893 --> 01:11:49.893
to different modes of
entertainment can fit in that box

01:11:52.380 --> 01:11:54.730
and it's not clear, at least to me,

01:11:54.730 --> 01:11:59.730
how that very diverse set of
factors might have an impact.

01:12:03.000 --> 01:12:04.856
So those are my two comments.

01:12:04.856 --> 01:12:06.963
I'll send you written comments as well

01:12:06.963 --> 01:12:09.700
on the whole paper.

01:12:09.700 --> 01:12:10.533
Thanks.

01:12:12.010 --> 01:12:13.230
- Great, thank you.

01:12:13.230 --> 01:12:14.730
Next, we've Phillip Lipscy

01:12:14.730 --> 01:12:17.050
and then Michael Thies after that.

01:12:17.050 --> 01:12:19.220
- Yeah, I really like this paper.

01:12:19.220 --> 01:12:20.670
Thanks for the opportunity to read it.

01:12:20.670 --> 01:12:22.910
So I had two comments.

01:12:22.910 --> 01:12:23.743
I have a bunch more

01:12:23.743 --> 01:12:25.620
but I'll just focus on two.

01:12:25.620 --> 01:12:26.850
So, so one of them is similar

01:12:26.850 --> 01:12:31.750
to what Atsushi was saying
in his discussing comments,

01:12:31.750 --> 01:12:34.300
which is I felt like the paper might need

01:12:34.300 --> 01:12:36.850
some kind of theory about how existing

01:12:36.850 --> 01:12:39.540
public diplomacy efforts relate

01:12:39.540 --> 01:12:42.660
to the methodology, right?

01:12:42.660 --> 01:12:46.279
In other words, if Japan is very active

01:12:46.279 --> 01:12:49.340
in public diplomacy efforts in a country,

01:12:49.340 --> 01:12:51.690
is your treatment effect
going to be attenuated

01:12:51.690 --> 01:12:55.160
because citizens are already
being aggressively primed

01:12:55.160 --> 01:12:57.410
with the same kind of information?

01:12:57.410 --> 01:13:00.330
And does the degree of public diplomacy

01:13:00.330 --> 01:13:03.540
differ between Korea and
Malaysia, for example?

01:13:03.540 --> 01:13:05.210
The assumption seems to be,

01:13:05.210 --> 01:13:07.380
it's going to be the
same, there was no effect,

01:13:07.380 --> 01:13:09.451
but is that really true?

01:13:09.451 --> 01:13:11.676
I mean, the other possibility is that

01:13:11.676 --> 01:13:13.840
through these existing efforts,

01:13:13.840 --> 01:13:15.800
citizens would become more susceptible

01:13:15.800 --> 01:13:16.980
to prime somehow,

01:13:16.980 --> 01:13:19.210
so that might be another possibility.

01:13:19.210 --> 01:13:20.430
But in either case,

01:13:20.430 --> 01:13:23.270
I think some discussion
of that would be useful.

01:13:23.270 --> 01:13:26.025
And the second point was,

01:13:26.025 --> 01:13:30.273
my observation of the
public diplomacy strategy,

01:13:30.273 --> 01:13:34.610
as it's occurring, seems
to be focused lately

01:13:34.610 --> 01:13:36.280
on opinion leaders

01:13:36.280 --> 01:13:39.660
and I've even heard influencers used

01:13:39.660 --> 01:13:41.460
by some officials,

01:13:41.460 --> 01:13:44.740
and sort of thinking about
that mechanism, right?

01:13:44.740 --> 01:13:46.600
So if these messages are coming

01:13:46.600 --> 01:13:48.800
from people you trust

01:13:48.800 --> 01:13:50.730
within your own country,

01:13:50.730 --> 01:13:52.220
does that make a difference

01:13:52.220 --> 01:13:54.350
versus if it's obviously coming

01:13:54.350 --> 01:13:56.480
from the source country?

01:13:56.480 --> 01:13:58.813
Because that seems to be, at least,

01:13:59.821 --> 01:14:01.060
from my very qualitative,

01:14:01.060 --> 01:14:02.510
impressionistic sense,

01:14:02.510 --> 01:14:04.630
where a lot of the attention is going

01:14:04.630 --> 01:14:06.780
in the public diplomacy sphere these days.

01:14:06.780 --> 01:14:08.070
It's let's focus on these people

01:14:08.070 --> 01:14:09.700
who have a lot of Twitter followers

01:14:09.700 --> 01:14:11.970
and try to change their mind.

01:14:11.970 --> 01:14:12.930
So for future research,

01:14:12.930 --> 01:14:14.620
that might be worth thinking about.

01:14:14.620 --> 01:14:15.453
Thanks.

01:14:17.098 --> 01:14:20.020
- Great, thank you.

01:14:20.020 --> 01:14:21.193
Michael Thies is next.

01:14:24.630 --> 01:14:26.290
- Ah, thanks.

01:14:26.290 --> 01:14:29.260
Yeah, thank you for the
presentation going this late.

01:14:29.260 --> 01:14:31.913
Some of the points I wanted to
make have already been made,

01:14:31.913 --> 01:14:34.403
particularly by Jennifer and Ben.

01:14:36.720 --> 01:14:40.850
So let me try to summarize what I see

01:14:40.850 --> 01:14:44.220
as the source of some of these concerns,

01:14:44.220 --> 01:14:48.300
and that is that while
you run the experiment

01:14:48.300 --> 01:14:49.240
in the way you should

01:14:49.240 --> 01:14:52.350
and you check for
balance within your cases

01:14:52.350 --> 01:14:53.330
in all the right ways,

01:14:53.330 --> 01:14:55.050
and you have enough
presumably enough power

01:14:55.050 --> 01:14:57.890
to make the inferences you want to make

01:14:57.890 --> 01:14:59.720
within the cases,

01:14:59.720 --> 01:15:02.730
for the big points that
you're making in the paper,

01:15:02.730 --> 01:15:06.610
what you really have
is an N of two, right?

01:15:06.610 --> 01:15:08.410
You've got South Korea, Malaysia

01:15:08.410 --> 01:15:11.240
and I guess two senders as well.

01:15:11.240 --> 01:15:13.520
And a lot of the questions, I think,

01:15:13.520 --> 01:15:17.430
come from the notion that
you acknowledged in the paper

01:15:17.430 --> 01:15:19.530
that there are a lot of differences

01:15:19.530 --> 01:15:20.891
between these countries

01:15:20.891 --> 01:15:24.022
that may be confounding

01:15:24.022 --> 01:15:28.087
at least your interpretations
of the results

01:15:28.087 --> 01:15:30.020
that you're finding.

01:15:30.020 --> 01:15:32.010
So one thing that struck me

01:15:32.010 --> 01:15:34.960
on the aid treatment in particular is

01:15:34.960 --> 01:15:37.740
that I just looked and, at
least according to the IMF,

01:15:37.740 --> 01:15:40.050
South Korea now has a
higher per capita income

01:15:40.050 --> 01:15:41.445
than Japan,

01:15:41.445 --> 01:15:43.820
and much, much higher
than China, of course.

01:15:43.820 --> 01:15:46.450
So aid, who knows how aid plays,

01:15:46.450 --> 01:15:48.280
and particularly Japanese aid plays,

01:15:48.280 --> 01:15:50.760
in the Korean sort of imagination?

01:15:50.760 --> 01:15:51.810
Is it, is it envy?

01:15:51.810 --> 01:15:52.643
Is it jealousy?

01:15:52.643 --> 01:15:53.550
Is it resentment?

01:15:53.550 --> 01:15:55.360
What's going on?

01:15:55.360 --> 01:15:56.610
There's a lot there.

01:15:56.610 --> 01:15:59.330
And then, South Korea
is smack in the middle

01:15:59.330 --> 01:16:00.710
of Japan and China.

01:16:00.710 --> 01:16:03.853
It's affected by hard
power in lots of ways.

01:16:07.100 --> 01:16:08.463
It's different from
Malaysia in so many ways

01:16:08.463 --> 01:16:10.358
that it's hard to isolate

01:16:10.358 --> 01:16:13.440
the thing that's really
causing the difference.

01:16:13.440 --> 01:16:16.760
Is it this politicization of
the bilateral relationship

01:16:16.760 --> 01:16:18.023
or is it something else?

01:16:19.460 --> 01:16:20.630
On the politicization,

01:16:20.630 --> 01:16:23.335
I will echo the points made earlier,

01:16:23.335 --> 01:16:26.390
that I think it would be useful for you,

01:16:26.390 --> 01:16:28.223
at the very least in the conclusion,

01:16:29.470 --> 01:16:33.440
to suggest more systematic ways

01:16:33.440 --> 01:16:36.094
to measure politicization.

01:16:36.094 --> 01:16:38.763
So I think you started
with these two cases,

01:16:38.763 --> 01:16:41.210
Korean and Malaysia,
where you just figured

01:16:41.210 --> 01:16:42.710
this is going to be uncontroversial.

01:16:42.710 --> 01:16:43.700
Everyone's going to agree

01:16:43.700 --> 01:16:46.140
that the relationships between Korea

01:16:46.140 --> 01:16:51.080
and the two senders
are clearly politicized

01:16:51.080 --> 01:16:52.960
and for Malaysia, there doesn't seem

01:16:52.960 --> 01:16:54.709
to be any evidence of that.

01:16:54.709 --> 01:16:56.390
It'd be nice.

01:16:56.390 --> 01:16:57.920
It's always...

01:16:57.920 --> 01:16:59.880
Professor Nye use the word "churlish."

01:16:59.880 --> 01:17:03.867
It's always difficult to
comment on an experiment to say,

01:17:03.867 --> 01:17:06.305
"Well, here's all the things
you should have done."

01:17:06.305 --> 01:17:09.976
But to have (indistinct)
diagonal cases, right?

01:17:09.976 --> 01:17:12.560
Where one of them had a
politicized relationship

01:17:12.560 --> 01:17:13.990
with China but not with Japan

01:17:13.990 --> 01:17:15.100
and the other vice versa,

01:17:15.100 --> 01:17:17.540
if there are any such cases,

01:17:17.540 --> 01:17:19.540
to get a little bit of leverage on that.

01:17:20.447 --> 01:17:23.160
I also wanted to echo what Jennifer said

01:17:23.160 --> 01:17:24.834
about the treatment of culture.

01:17:24.834 --> 01:17:28.423
In Korea, to my understanding,

01:17:31.824 --> 01:17:34.660
culture gets hijacked in the politics

01:17:34.660 --> 01:17:36.759
of the relationship
between the two countries.

01:17:36.759 --> 01:17:39.930
There are no particular
cultural disputes specifically

01:17:39.930 --> 01:17:44.930
because culture and industry,
money are so related,

01:17:46.428 --> 01:17:49.759
South Korean government
likes to use culture

01:17:49.759 --> 01:17:51.476
as sort of a weapon

01:17:51.476 --> 01:17:55.260
in it's political disputes with Japan.

01:17:55.260 --> 01:17:59.417
So is the fraught nature
of their relationship,

01:17:59.417 --> 01:18:00.690
the politicization of the relationship,

01:18:00.690 --> 01:18:02.020
really about culture

01:18:02.020 --> 01:18:04.406
or is cultures just being used there?

01:18:04.406 --> 01:18:05.900
And so, is it really a good case

01:18:05.900 --> 01:18:08.800
for finding the impact of
the cultural treatments

01:18:09.720 --> 01:18:10.920
that you're looking for?

01:18:12.380 --> 01:18:15.400
Again, to echo Professor Nye's point

01:18:15.400 --> 01:18:17.430
about time series.

01:18:17.430 --> 01:18:21.390
You do mention COVID
is as a potential issue

01:18:21.390 --> 01:18:24.070
for doing this in February of '21,

01:18:24.070 --> 01:18:27.680
but just to snapshot is going to be

01:18:27.680 --> 01:18:30.253
a bit of an issue because diplomacy is

01:18:30.253 --> 01:18:31.430
a continuous thing.

01:18:31.430 --> 01:18:33.140
It waxes and wanes.

01:18:33.140 --> 01:18:35.360
South Korean and Japan, for example,

01:18:35.360 --> 01:18:37.680
or with China,

01:18:37.680 --> 01:18:39.600
the relationship warms and it cools

01:18:39.600 --> 01:18:40.660
and it warms and it cools.

01:18:40.660 --> 01:18:42.910
And so, if the questions
happened to be asked

01:18:42.910 --> 01:18:45.114
of people at a time when

01:18:45.114 --> 01:18:47.740
the South Korean government is explicitly

01:18:47.740 --> 01:18:50.680
or implicitly organizing a boycott

01:18:50.680 --> 01:18:52.010
of Japanese cultural products,

01:18:52.010 --> 01:18:53.935
you're gonna get a very different
set of answers, probably,

01:18:53.935 --> 01:18:55.610
from your respondents

01:18:55.610 --> 01:18:58.000
than if it's in a warmer period,

01:18:58.000 --> 01:18:58.833
for whatever.

01:19:01.810 --> 01:19:03.870
The last thing I wanted to say is,

01:19:03.870 --> 01:19:06.851
and here, Jennifer really did anticipate

01:19:06.851 --> 01:19:09.270
one of my suggestions,

01:19:09.270 --> 01:19:11.650
which is for those of us
who are not already steeped

01:19:11.650 --> 01:19:13.733
in the literature on soft power,

01:19:14.576 --> 01:19:18.380
a slightly more rigorous summary

01:19:18.380 --> 01:19:22.380
of that literature about why it matters

01:19:22.380 --> 01:19:24.770
and about how it works

01:19:27.340 --> 01:19:29.725
I think would be useful,

01:19:29.725 --> 01:19:30.650
especially if you want to send this

01:19:30.650 --> 01:19:33.283
to a general interest journal.

01:19:35.080 --> 01:19:37.150
And how it works, what I mean by that is,

01:19:37.150 --> 01:19:39.463
your measures looking at these surveys,

01:19:41.270 --> 01:19:42.590
and I guess this could be said about

01:19:42.590 --> 01:19:45.230
the other paper that
we'll discuss next too,

01:19:45.230 --> 01:19:49.010
are looking at the impact
on public opinion, right?

01:19:49.010 --> 01:19:50.820
Just the average citizen in the street.

01:19:50.820 --> 01:19:52.970
What are their feelings about this country

01:19:52.970 --> 01:19:55.240
in terms of trust or things like that?

01:19:55.240 --> 01:20:00.240
How does that translate to the goals

01:20:00.310 --> 01:20:02.670
that the sending country is

01:20:02.670 --> 01:20:04.930
actually seeking to fulfill?

01:20:04.930 --> 01:20:07.830
So how does a warm feeling about Japan

01:20:07.830 --> 01:20:11.060
or about China make its way up

01:20:11.060 --> 01:20:13.560
to your own government's relationship

01:20:13.560 --> 01:20:15.610
with the sending country?

01:20:15.610 --> 01:20:17.950
I'm sure this is in the literature already

01:20:17.950 --> 01:20:19.600
in general terms.

01:20:19.600 --> 01:20:24.600
but if you could summarize
that for the reader,

01:20:24.760 --> 01:20:25.900
but then also think about

01:20:25.900 --> 01:20:29.939
how you might get at that

01:20:29.939 --> 01:20:33.198
in terms of your examination.

01:20:33.198 --> 01:20:34.429
So this may be more of

01:20:34.429 --> 01:20:37.030
an observational accompaniment
to the experiment.

01:20:37.030 --> 01:20:41.259
But just as a comparativist
who doesn't study

01:20:41.259 --> 01:20:43.800
international relations and soft power,

01:20:43.800 --> 01:20:48.300
the "so what?" question really
was flashing over my head

01:20:48.300 --> 01:20:49.510
as I was reading.

01:20:49.510 --> 01:20:52.160
It's like, this is all
very interesting internally

01:20:52.160 --> 01:20:54.540
within the experiment but...

01:20:54.540 --> 01:20:56.247
Especially, when you were looking for

01:20:56.247 --> 01:20:59.320
the longevity of these things

01:20:59.320 --> 01:21:00.870
and you said you did a follow-up

01:21:00.870 --> 01:21:02.280
a week later or something like that,

01:21:02.280 --> 01:21:03.720
a couple of weeks later.

01:21:03.720 --> 01:21:06.860
And I think in the other paper,

01:21:06.860 --> 01:21:08.900
they showed results
that lasted three weeks

01:21:08.900 --> 01:21:10.470
or something like that.

01:21:10.470 --> 01:21:13.763
What's three weeks in a
diplomatic relationship?

01:21:15.740 --> 01:21:17.620
How big a deal is this?

01:21:17.620 --> 01:21:18.620
Some sense of that.

01:21:18.620 --> 01:21:20.910
So I'm not necessarily
saying it's not a big deal

01:21:20.910 --> 01:21:22.823
but sell it to the reader.

01:21:26.140 --> 01:21:26.973
- Great, thank you.

01:21:26.973 --> 01:21:29.703
And then finally, Sijeong Lim.

01:21:30.750 --> 01:21:32.820
And maybe, Tae-Ho Kim, you also...

01:21:33.853 --> 01:21:34.780
Okay.

01:21:34.780 --> 01:21:38.210
- Thank you for sharing your
fascinating research with us.

01:21:38.210 --> 01:21:41.016
I actually shared on many strong points

01:21:41.016 --> 01:21:42.683
about the paper,

01:21:42.683 --> 01:21:44.189
but I also share some of the concerns

01:21:44.189 --> 01:21:45.857
about the treatments.

01:21:45.857 --> 01:21:48.556
Whether the treatments are

01:21:48.556 --> 01:21:51.378
actually manipulating the things

01:21:51.378 --> 01:21:54.920
that the authors wanted to manipulate.

01:21:54.920 --> 01:21:56.635
And on that point,

01:21:56.635 --> 01:22:00.500
if you were to do a followup survey

01:22:00.500 --> 01:22:01.830
or redesign the survey,

01:22:01.830 --> 01:22:03.960
one thing I just wanted to suggest,

01:22:03.960 --> 01:22:06.680
as also one of the South Korean audience

01:22:06.680 --> 01:22:07.720
in the room,

01:22:07.720 --> 01:22:09.640
was to make the treatment sharper

01:22:09.640 --> 01:22:13.200
and, in doing so, also try
to make it more relational,

01:22:13.200 --> 01:22:15.270
more directed,

01:22:15.270 --> 01:22:20.270
and centering on the empathy.

01:22:20.750 --> 01:22:22.180
So in this treatment

01:22:22.180 --> 01:22:24.070
across all three issue areas,

01:22:24.070 --> 01:22:25.460
the treatments were videos.

01:22:25.460 --> 01:22:28.060
It doesn't have any particular target.

01:22:28.060 --> 01:22:30.300
It doesn't at least mention the target

01:22:30.300 --> 01:22:32.163
but I think what South Koreans

01:22:32.163 --> 01:22:34.630
especially appreciate when it comes

01:22:34.630 --> 01:22:36.270
to the relationship with Japan,

01:22:36.270 --> 01:22:38.640
especially the ordinary
public in South Korea,

01:22:38.640 --> 01:22:42.630
is how some of the
Japanese politicians are

01:22:42.630 --> 01:22:45.960
actually thinking similarly with Koreans.

01:22:45.960 --> 01:22:47.700
So maybe if there's
some of the treatments,

01:22:47.700 --> 01:22:49.310
maybe not in terms of the video,

01:22:49.310 --> 01:22:50.580
which is harder,

01:22:50.580 --> 01:22:52.250
but in just traditional vignettes,

01:22:52.250 --> 01:22:54.170
if somehow...

01:22:54.170 --> 01:22:56.930
For instance, the political
value treatment mentions

01:22:56.930 --> 01:22:59.390
how as democracy,

01:22:59.390 --> 01:23:02.370
we, Japan are quite similar to Korea

01:23:02.370 --> 01:23:05.396
in having this vibrant civil society,

01:23:05.396 --> 01:23:07.900
and that's where you can make

01:23:07.900 --> 01:23:11.180
the public diplomacy activity relational

01:23:11.180 --> 01:23:15.270
and resort to empathy.

01:23:15.270 --> 01:23:17.430
I think that's might be the key actually

01:23:17.430 --> 01:23:19.610
in how the public diplomacy
should be conducted

01:23:19.610 --> 01:23:23.870
in a more politicized
and a rivalry setting

01:23:23.870 --> 01:23:27.180
because, in that way,
you can see how Japan is

01:23:27.180 --> 01:23:29.850
also reaching out to us Koreans,

01:23:29.850 --> 01:23:31.840
not just to to the broader audience

01:23:31.840 --> 01:23:33.050
across the globe.

01:23:33.050 --> 01:23:34.150
So I think...

01:23:34.150 --> 01:23:37.180
I just wonder if the
more relational message,

01:23:37.180 --> 01:23:38.470
more directed message,

01:23:38.470 --> 01:23:39.820
have a different impact,

01:23:39.820 --> 01:23:42.811
especially if the modular
impact could be greater

01:23:42.811 --> 01:23:46.891
when under the conditions
of politicization.

01:23:46.891 --> 01:23:50.650
Also, about the measuring of

01:23:50.650 --> 01:23:51.680
the dependent variable.

01:23:51.680 --> 01:23:54.380
I was wondering if you can take advantage

01:23:54.380 --> 01:23:56.480
of the competition environment here

01:23:56.480 --> 01:23:58.270
between Japan and Korea,

01:23:58.270 --> 01:23:59.930
and whether you actually had a

01:23:59.930 --> 01:24:01.740
both choice type of DV,

01:24:01.740 --> 01:24:06.740
whether you will cooperate
more with China or Japan thing,

01:24:07.990 --> 01:24:11.200
instead of just asking them one-by-one

01:24:11.200 --> 01:24:14.080
and say, if this competition...

01:24:14.080 --> 01:24:16.710
Even though there is a slight backlash,

01:24:16.710 --> 01:24:19.960
maybe still, the recipient of

01:24:19.960 --> 01:24:22.620
the public diplomacy will choose us

01:24:22.620 --> 01:24:24.000
over the competitor.

01:24:24.000 --> 01:24:26.290
So I just wanted to see how

01:24:26.290 --> 01:24:29.030
this competition dynamic
might be incorporated

01:24:29.030 --> 01:24:30.650
into the survey design,

01:24:30.650 --> 01:24:33.200
especially in the East Asian context.

01:24:33.200 --> 01:24:34.050
That's it for me.

01:24:36.030 --> 01:24:36.863
- Great, thank you.

01:24:36.863 --> 01:24:39.290
And last, Tae-Ho Kim.

01:24:39.290 --> 01:24:44.190
- Thank you, I really
enjoyed good comments.

01:24:44.190 --> 01:24:48.520
And just to try to add two small points;

01:24:48.520 --> 01:24:51.550
one about case selection

01:24:51.550 --> 01:24:54.713
and second one, a policy recommendation.

01:24:56.070 --> 01:25:01.070
I think the motivation
to start this paper comes

01:25:04.090 --> 01:25:06.753
from Japan-China regional rivalry.

01:25:07.620 --> 01:25:12.390
Hard power rivalry between one US ally

01:25:12.390 --> 01:25:15.510
and one competitor of US.

01:25:15.510 --> 01:25:17.636
And then, you became curious

01:25:17.636 --> 01:25:22.636
how these two countries
soft power works for Korea,

01:25:24.470 --> 01:25:27.010
which means you picked up case

01:25:27.010 --> 01:25:29.290
out of strong hard power,

01:25:29.290 --> 01:25:31.440
and then try to relate soft power

01:25:31.440 --> 01:25:33.863
to explain your hard power relationship.

01:25:34.730 --> 01:25:39.730
But reality, South Korea is a
more strong soft power sender

01:25:40.540 --> 01:25:42.405
to China and Japan

01:25:42.405 --> 01:25:46.990
in K-pops and sports and cultures.

01:25:46.990 --> 01:25:50.130
And China controls Korean soft power,

01:25:50.130 --> 01:25:52.870
and Japan just a little bit to play

01:25:52.870 --> 01:25:54.920
in domestic politics.

01:25:54.920 --> 01:25:57.630
So I think you should think about

01:25:57.630 --> 01:26:00.552
the influence of soft power of South Korea

01:26:00.552 --> 01:26:03.030
to these great powers.

01:26:03.030 --> 01:26:06.550
If you try to academically compare

01:26:06.550 --> 01:26:08.935
the role of soft and hard power.

01:26:08.935 --> 01:26:12.174
My second point is about the solution

01:26:12.174 --> 01:26:14.940
that this paper is very strong

01:26:14.940 --> 01:26:16.590
in the deductive logic.

01:26:16.590 --> 01:26:21.337
The key is a politicization
in domestic politics.

01:26:21.337 --> 01:26:26.337
But the problem is South
Korea's domestic politicization

01:26:26.520 --> 01:26:28.309
on history issue.

01:26:28.309 --> 01:26:30.450
Here, the key is,

01:26:30.450 --> 01:26:33.780
who captured the power
in South Korean politics?

01:26:33.780 --> 01:26:36.025
If you look at the past 20 years history,

01:26:36.025 --> 01:26:37.590
whenever conservative, righty,

01:26:37.590 --> 01:26:41.960
political camp captures Korean power,

01:26:41.960 --> 01:26:44.558
Korea-Japan relations get ease

01:26:44.558 --> 01:26:48.909
and much more co-operative
in security issues.

01:26:48.909 --> 01:26:53.460
But like this time,

01:26:53.460 --> 01:26:56.309
liberal and lefty
governments captures power,

01:26:56.309 --> 01:27:00.300
all problems becomes politicized.

01:27:00.300 --> 01:27:04.730
So this paper implies
Korean domestic politics,

01:27:04.730 --> 01:27:07.470
which cannot be controlled
by Japan and China,

01:27:07.470 --> 01:27:10.490
will decide the impact of hard power

01:27:10.490 --> 01:27:13.550
and soft power relationship
between Japan and Korea...

01:27:13.550 --> 01:27:17.630
Between Korea and China
and Korea and Japan.

01:27:17.630 --> 01:27:18.630
Thank you very much.

01:27:21.719 --> 01:27:22.552
- Great work.

01:27:22.552 --> 01:27:24.580
Thank you to all of the panel participants

01:27:24.580 --> 01:27:25.810
and especially to our discussants,

01:27:25.810 --> 01:27:26.643
Professor Joe Nye,

01:27:26.643 --> 01:27:28.400
professor Maria Repnikova,

01:27:28.400 --> 01:27:29.810
Professor Atsushi Tago

01:27:29.810 --> 01:27:31.740
and Professor Tetsuro Kobayashi

01:27:31.740 --> 01:27:32.940
for those fantastic comments.

01:27:32.940 --> 01:27:34.650
I'm again going to resist the urge

01:27:34.650 --> 01:27:36.550
to give a lengthy response

01:27:36.550 --> 01:27:39.210
to these great, great suggestions

01:27:40.160 --> 01:27:44.720
and points to push our
thinking further along.

01:27:44.720 --> 01:27:47.470
So I think that whether
it's thinking carefully

01:27:47.470 --> 01:27:49.370
about what the treatments
are manipulating,

01:27:49.370 --> 01:27:51.120
what's (indistinct) giving us,

01:27:51.120 --> 01:27:51.953
what's something we can clarify.

01:27:51.953 --> 01:27:54.380
To these bigger questions about the theory

01:27:54.380 --> 01:27:55.890
that the paper is trying to develop,

01:27:55.890 --> 01:27:58.810
I mean, I think, as Professor Nye

01:27:58.810 --> 01:28:00.820
and Repnikova grip and a
bunch of people suggested,

01:28:00.820 --> 01:28:04.514
thinking over time and how this changes

01:28:04.514 --> 01:28:07.780
and how, as Professor Catalinac suggested,

01:28:07.780 --> 01:28:09.490
how governments are
actually doing this can help

01:28:09.490 --> 01:28:10.830
to answer this question

01:28:10.830 --> 01:28:12.591
that Mike Tomz asked about,

01:28:12.591 --> 01:28:14.817
"What's theoretically interesting...

01:28:14.817 --> 01:28:17.060
"What's theoretically interesting here?"

01:28:17.060 --> 01:28:20.037
So this has given us
great food for thought.

01:28:20.037 --> 01:28:20.905
I really appreciate it.

01:28:20.905 --> 01:28:21.738
I'm mindful however

01:28:21.738 --> 01:28:22.747
that everyone was promised a Zoom break.

01:28:22.747 --> 01:28:26.705
And so, I do want to stick to that

01:28:26.705 --> 01:28:31.070
while still giving the next
paper presenters ample time

01:28:31.070 --> 01:28:34.740
to discuss their really
excellent, exciting paper.

01:28:34.740 --> 01:28:39.040
So as planned, let's try
to come back at 08:40.

01:28:39.040 --> 01:28:41.240
So it gives us a slightly
shorter break than planned

01:28:41.240 --> 01:28:43.020
but still maybe a little
time to walk around

01:28:43.020 --> 01:28:46.060
and get away from our screens.

01:28:46.060 --> 01:28:47.420
So, thanks again so much

01:28:47.420 --> 01:28:49.450
and I'll see everybody
in about eight minutes.

01:28:49.450 --> 01:28:50.283
Thank you.

